{"qid": 0, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "The implied warranty of merchantability (UCC \u00a7 2-314) arises automatically when a merchant sells goods and guarantees they are fit for their ordinary purposes and safe; the buyer can invoke it even absent express warranties or a warranty of fitness for a particular purpose.\n\nFINAL: The implied warranty of merchantability", "final": "The implied warranty of merchantability"}
{"qid": 1, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "The facts describe goods that are not fit for their ordinary purposes and sold by a merchant, which is precisely what the UCC's implied warranty of merchantability (UCC \u00a7 2-314) covers; no express warranty or particular-purpose reliance is stated, so the implied warranty of merchantability is the one the buyer can invoke.\n\nFINAL: The implied warranty of merchantability (UCC \u00a7 2-314), since a merchant seller impliedly warrants goods are fit for their ordinary purposes.", "final": "The implied warranty of merchantability (UCC \u00a7 2-314), since a merchant seller impliedly warrants goods are fit for their ordinary purposes."}
{"qid": 2, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic MBE question; the strongest defense is the market participant exception: the state, when spending its own funds on construction contracts, acts as a market participant rather than a regulator, so the dormant commerce clause does not apply. Therefore the answer is that the statute applies only to state-funded construction contracts where the state is acting as a market participant (e.g., \"the state's decision to hire only its own citizens is made in its capacity as a purchaser/market participant\").\n\nFINAL: The state is acting as a market participant (spending its own funds on state construction contracts), so the dormant commerce clause does not restrict its preference for in-state citizens.", "final": "The state is acting as a market participant (spending its own funds on state construction contracts), so the dormant commerce clause does not restrict its preference for in-state citizens."}
{"qid": 3, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic MBE question; the answer choices typically include: (A) the statute will help protect the workers of the state from competition by foreign workers; (B) the statute will help ensure that workers with contacts in the state will be available; (C) the statute will help ensure a continuously available and stable workforce for the execution of public contracts; (D) if the state uses its own tax funds to finance state construction projects, it is free to define the terms for hiring. The strongest commerce clause defense is the market participant exception \u2014 when the state spends its own funds as a purchaser of construction services, it may favor its own citizens without violating the dormant commerce clause (Reeves v. Stake; White v. Massachusetts Council of Construction Employers).\n\nFINAL: The state is acting as a market participant\u2014since it uses its own tax funds to finance the construction projects, it is free to define the terms (including hiring only state citizens) on which it will do business, so the dormant commerce clause does not apply.", "final": "The state is acting as a market participant\u2014since it uses its own tax funds to finance the construction projects, it is free to define the terms (including hiring only state citizens) on which it will do business, so the dormant commerce clause does not apply."}
{"qid": 4, "answered": null, "resp": "Under FRCP 32(a)(3), an adverse party may use the deposition of a party for any purpose at trial, regardless of the deponent's availability; also, March's deposition statements are admissions by a party-opponent under FRE 801(d)(2). Thus Cooper may read the relevant portions of March's deposition without calling March to testify.\n\nFINAL: Cooper may use the deposition; the objection will be overruled because a party's deposition may be used by an adverse party for any purpose (FRCP 32(a)(3)) and constitutes a party-opponent admission, regardless of March's availability to testify.", "final": "Cooper may use the deposition; the objection will be overruled because a party's deposition may be used by an adverse party for any purpose (FRCP 32(a)(3)) and constitutes a party-opponent admission, regardless of March's availability to testify."}
{"qid": 5, "answered": null, "resp": "Polly's statement is offered against her by the opposing party (Justin's estate), so it qualifies as a party-opponent admission under FRE 801(d)(2)(A) and is not hearsay; opinion or legal-conclusion rules do not bar such admissions, and it is relevant to negligence.\n\nFINAL: Yes\u2014the statement is admissible as a party-opponent admission (non-hearsay under FRE 801(d)(2)(A)), including the \"I was negligent\" portion, since personal-knowledge and opinion limits don't apply to party admissions.", "final": "Yes\u2014the statement is admissible as a party-opponent admission (non-hearsay under FRE 801(d)(2)(A)), including the \"I was negligent\" portion, since personal-knowledge and opinion limits don't apply to party admissions."}
{"qid": 6, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic MBE question: the witness's prior silence when asked directly by police whether the defendant mentioned an alibi is inconsistent with her trial testimony, so it is proper impeachment by a prior inconsistent statement (silence/omission counting as inconsistency where one would naturally have spoken). The cross-examination is proper as impeachment of the witness.\n\nFINAL: Yes, because the witness's failure to mention the statement when asked by police is a prior inconsistent statement (by omission) that may be used to impeach her credibility.", "final": "Yes, because the witness's failure to mention the statement when asked by police is a prior inconsistent statement (by omission) that may be used to impeach her credibility."}
{"qid": 7, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic MBE question; the answer choices typically are: (A) prevail, because a special duty of care was owed him; (B) prevail, because the sudden stop caused the resident to fall; (C) not prevail, if the operator was not negligent in making the stop; (D) not prevail, because the resident was the direct cause. The correct answer: the commuter will not prevail if the subway operator was not negligent in making the abrupt stop, since common carriers owe a high duty of care but are not strictly liable\u2014negligence must still be shown.\n\nFINAL: Not prevail, if the subway operator was not negligent in making the abrupt stop (a common carrier owes a high degree of care but is not strictly liable, so the commuter must prove negligence).", "final": "Not prevail, if the subway operator was not negligent in making the abrupt stop (a common carrier owes a high degree of care but is not strictly liable, so the commuter must prove negligence)."}
{"qid": 8, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "This appears to reference Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 45(a)(1)(A)(iii), which allows a subpoena to command attendance and testimony; production of documents, electronically stored information, or tangible things; or permitting inspection of premises. A subpoena cannot command a person to submit to a physical or mental examination \u2014 that requires a court order under Rule 35.\n\nFINAL: Submit to a physical or mental examination (a subpoena may only command testimony, production of documents/ESI/tangible things, or inspection of premises).", "final": "Submit to a physical or mental examination (a subpoena may only command testimony, production of documents/ESI/tangible things, or inspection of premises)."}
{"qid": 9, "answered": null, "resp": "This mirrors Bigelow v. Virginia (1975), where the Supreme Court held that a paid advertisement conveying information about legal abortion services in another state is protected commercial speech under the First Amendment, and the state cannot criminalize its dissemination. Therefore, the conviction will not be upheld.\n\nFINAL: No \u2014 the conviction violates the First Amendment; the advertisement is protected speech (Bigelow v. Virginia), so the courts will reverse it.", "final": "No \u2014 the conviction violates the First Amendment; the advertisement is protected speech (Bigelow v. Virginia), so the courts will reverse it."}
{"qid": 10, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic bar-exam question: paying half the salaries of teachers in private (including parochial) schools violates the Establishment Clause under Lemon v. Kurtzman because it fosters excessive government entanglement with religion (the state would have to monitor teachers to ensure secular instruction). The taxpayers have standing under Flast v. Cohen to bring an Establishment Clause challenge to a taxing/spending measure.\n\nFINAL: Unconstitutional, because it violates the Establishment Clause of the First Amendment by creating excessive government entanglement with religion (Lemon v. Kurtzman); the taxpayers have standing under Flast v. Cohen.", "final": "Unconstitutional, because it violates the Establishment Clause of the First Amendment by creating excessive government entanglement with religion (Lemon v. Kurtzman); the taxpayers have standing under Flast v. Cohen."}
{"qid": 11, "answered": null, "resp": "The question lists no options, but historically the revival of natural law in the 20th century was driven chiefly by the atrocities of World War II and the Nazi regime, which exposed the moral inadequacy of legal positivism and led to the Nuremberg trials, the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, and renewed interest in objective moral standards above positive law (e.g., Radbruch, Fuller, Finnis).\n\nFINAL: The atrocities of World War II/Nazi Germany (and the resulting Nuremberg trials and human rights movement), which exposed the inadequacy of legal positivism and prompted a return to higher moral standards for law.", "final": "The atrocities of World War II/Nazi Germany (and the resulting Nuremberg trials and human rights movement), which exposed the inadequacy of legal positivism and prompted a return to higher moral standards for law."}
{"qid": 12, "answered": null, "resp": "Austin's imperative theory defines law as a command of the sovereign backed by sanction, so its attributes are command, sovereign, sanction (and duty).\n\nFINAL: Law is a command of the sovereign, imposing a duty and backed by a sanction (command, sovereign, duty, sanction).", "final": "Law is a command of the sovereign, imposing a duty and backed by a sanction (command, sovereign, duty, sanction)."}
{"qid": 13, "answered": null, "resp": "Justiciability refers to whether a matter is suitable and appropriate for a court to hear and decide \u2014 i.e., whether it presents a genuine, concrete legal dispute that the judiciary can properly resolve. Doctrines such as standing, ripeness, mootness, the political question doctrine, and the prohibition on advisory opinions determine whether an issue is justiciable.\n\nFINAL: Justiciability is the quality of a matter being appropriate and capable of being decided by a court \u2014 a real, concrete legal dispute the judiciary can properly resolve (as opposed to hypothetical, moot, or political questions), determined by doctrines like standing, ripeness, mootness, and the political question doctrine.", "final": "Justiciability is the quality of a matter being appropriate and capable of being decided by a court \u2014 a real, concrete legal dispute the judiciary can properly resolve (as opposed to hypothetical, moot, or political questions), determined by doctrines like standing, ripeness, mootness, and the political question doctrine."}
{"qid": 14, "answered": null, "resp": "R2P, endorsed by the UN in 2005, reframes the issue from a right of outside states to intervene militarily to a responsibility\u2014primarily of the state itself and then of the international community\u2014to protect populations from genocide, war crimes, ethnic cleansing and crimes against humanity, emphasizing prevention, assistance, and a spectrum of non-military measures, with coercive force only as a last resort authorized by the UN Security Council. Humanitarian intervention, by contrast, is centered on unilateral or coalition military action justified by the intervening states' claimed right to act, often without UN authorization.\n\nFINAL: R2P is broader and prevention-focused, framing the issue as a responsibility (first of the state, then of the international community) to protect populations through diplomatic, humanitarian and other peaceful means, with military force only as a last resort authorized by the UN Security Council; humanitarian intervention is narrower, centered on a claimed right of states to use military force, often without UN authorization.", "final": "R2P is broader and prevention-focused, framing the issue as a responsibility (first of the state, then of the international community) to protect populations through diplomatic, humanitarian and other peaceful means, with military force only as a last resort authorized by the UN Security Council; humanitarian intervention is narrower, centered on a claimed right of states to use military force, often without UN authorization."}
{"qid": 15, "answered": null, "resp": "No. International tribunals' jurisdiction rests on state consent (e.g., compromissory clauses, special agreements, optional clause declarations) rather than on territorial sovereignty or compulsory jurisdiction as with national courts, though some general principles (like competence-competence) apply to both.\n\nFINAL: No \u2014 international tribunals' jurisdiction is fundamentally based on the consent of states, unlike the compulsory, sovereignty-based jurisdiction of national courts, so different principles apply.", "final": "No \u2014 international tribunals' jurisdiction is fundamentally based on the consent of states, unlike the compulsory, sovereignty-based jurisdiction of national courts, so different principles apply."}
{"qid": 16, "answered": null, "resp": "Under Article 19 of the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties (1969), a reservation is impermissible (invalid) when it is prohibited by the treaty, when the treaty permits only specified reservations which do not include the one in question, or when it is incompatible with the object and purpose of the treaty. Such invalid reservations are without legal effect and cannot be accepted by other parties.\n\nFINAL: A reservation is invalid when it is prohibited by the treaty, when it is not among the specific reservations the treaty permits, or when it is incompatible with the object and purpose of the treaty (Art. 19 VCLT).", "final": "A reservation is invalid when it is prohibited by the treaty, when it is not among the specific reservations the treaty permits, or when it is incompatible with the object and purpose of the treaty (Art. 19 VCLT)."}
{"qid": 17, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic MBE-style question about firm offers under UCC 2-205 and option contracts; the options aren't given, but the typical unenforceable one is an oral or unsigned promise by a merchant to keep an offer open (e.g., a merchant telephones a buyer offering widgets and says the offer will remain open for a week), since a firm offer requires a signed writing and, absent consideration, an oral promise to hold the offer open is freely revocable before acceptance.\n\nFINAL: The oral (non-written/unsigned) offer in which the merchant seller merely promises to keep the offer open without consideration \u2014 e.g., \"A merchant telephones a buyer, offering to sell widgets and stating the offer will remain open for a week\" \u2014 is revocable and not enforceable if the seller changes his mind before acceptance, because a UCC firm offer must be in a signed writing and an option requires consideration.", "final": "The oral (non-written/unsigned) offer in which the merchant seller merely promises to keep the offer open without consideration \u2014 e.g., \"A merchant telephones a buyer, offering to sell widgets and stating the offer will remain open for a week\" \u2014 is revocable and not enforceable if the seller changes his mind before acceptance, because a UCC firm offer must be in a signed writing and an option requires consideration."}
{"qid": 18, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic MBE question about firm offers under UCC 2-205 and option contracts; the typical answer choices include a merchant's signed written offer promising to hold open (firm offer, enforceable), an offer supported by consideration (option, enforceable), and a merchant's written offer that simply gives a time limit without a promise of irrevocability, or an oral promise to keep open, which is revocable. Without the listed options, the offer that remains revocable is one lacking both consideration and a signed written assurance by a merchant to hold it open\u2014e.g., a merchant's oral (or unsigned) statement that the offer will remain open for a period, or a written offer that merely states an expiration date (\"this offer expires in 10 days\") without promising not to revoke.\n\nFINAL: The offer that is not supported by consideration and is not a signed written merchant's firm-offer assurance\u2014e.g., a merchant's oral promise to hold the offer open (or a written offer that merely sets a lapse date without promising irrevocability)\u2014is freely revocable and thus not enforceable if the seller changes his mind before acceptance.", "final": "The offer that is not supported by consideration and is not a signed written merchant's firm-offer assurance\u2014e.g., a merchant's oral promise to hold the offer open (or a written offer that merely sets a lapse date without promising irrevocability)\u2014is freely revocable and thus not enforceable if the seller changes his mind before acceptance."}
{"qid": 19, "answered": null, "resp": "Yes. Malice aforethought for murder does not require a specific intent to kill; it can be established by intent to inflict serious bodily harm or by conduct showing extreme recklessness/depraved indifference to human life, and stabbing someone in the chest with a large serrated hunting knife demonstrates at least the intent to cause grievous bodily harm and a reckless disregard for life, so the claimed lack of intent to kill is not a defense.\n\nFINAL: Yes \u2014 the defendant is guilty of murder because malice may be implied from the intent to inflict serious bodily harm or from extreme recklessness toward human life (stabbing the victim in the chest with a large hunting knife), so intent to kill is not required.", "final": "Yes \u2014 the defendant is guilty of murder because malice may be implied from the intent to inflict serious bodily harm or from extreme recklessness toward human life (stabbing the victim in the chest with a large hunting knife), so intent to kill is not required."}
{"qid": 20, "answered": null, "resp": "Yes. Even absent specific intent to kill, stabbing someone in the chest with a large serrated hunting knife demonstrates malice aforethought through intent to inflict serious bodily harm or a depraved-heart/extreme recklessness disregard for human life, which suffices for murder; his claimed intent merely to scare is implausible given the act.\n\nFINAL: Yes, the defendant is guilty of murder because malice aforethought can be established by intent to inflict serious bodily harm or extreme recklessness toward human life, and stabbing the victim in the chest with a large hunting knife satisfies that standard regardless of his claimed intent only to scare.", "final": "Yes, the defendant is guilty of murder because malice aforethought can be established by intent to inflict serious bodily harm or extreme recklessness toward human life, and stabbing the victim in the chest with a large hunting knife satisfies that standard regardless of his claimed intent only to scare."}
{"qid": 21, "answered": null, "resp": "The question references answer options (\"which of the following\") that were not provided, so I can only describe the key facts: slander is spoken (not written) defamation, is generally not actionable without proof of special damages unless it falls into a per se category (imputing crime, loathsome disease, unchastity, or unfitness in trade/profession), and truth is a complete defense. Any statement contradicting these\u2014most commonly the claim that slander is written or that it is always actionable without proof of damage\u2014would be the \"not true\" choice.\n\nFINAL: Without the answer choices I cannot select one, but the false statement is likely that \"slander is written defamation\" (slander is spoken; libel is written) or that \"slander is always actionable without proof of special damages\" (only slander per se is).", "final": "Without the answer choices I cannot select one, but the false statement is likely that \"slander is written defamation\" (slander is spoken; libel is written) or that \"slander is always actionable without proof of special damages\" (only slander per se is)."}
{"qid": 22, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic unilateral contract question: under the modern rule (Restatement (Second) \u00a7 45), once the offeree begins performance of the requested act, an option contract is created and the offer becomes irrevocable, and beginning performance is distinguished from mere preparation. Buying gasoline and wheeling the mower over are preparations, but starting the mower on the man's property arguably marks the beginning of performance (the standard answer to this MBE-style question is that the neighbor does recover, because he began performance before revocation, though many treat the starting of the engine as still preparatory, in which case he would not).\n\nFINAL: Yes \u2014 under the modern rule, once the neighbor began performing (starting the mower on the man's property), the offer became irrevocable, so if he completes mowing the lawn he recovers the $50 (if the engine start were deemed mere preparation, the answer would be no; but the better view is that performance had begun).", "final": "Yes \u2014 under the modern rule, once the neighbor began performing (starting the mower on the man's property), the offer became irrevocable, so if he completes mowing the lawn he recovers the $50 (if the engine start were deemed mere preparation, the answer would be no; but the better view is that performance had begun)."}
{"qid": 23, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic bar exam question: Congress's taxing power is broad, and a tax will be upheld if it raises revenue (or is reasonably related to revenue raising) regardless of any regulatory motive or effect, and Congress need not show any other enumerated power (like commerce). The handgun tax does raise revenue, so it is valid under the Taxing and Spending Clause; also, it does not violate the Second Amendment given its modest amount and rational basis.\n\nFINAL: Yes \u2014 the tax will be upheld because it raises revenue (or is reasonably related to raising revenue), and Congress's taxing power is valid regardless of any regulatory purpose or effect on handgun sales.", "final": "Yes \u2014 the tax will be upheld because it raises revenue (or is reasonably related to raising revenue), and Congress's taxing power is valid regardless of any regulatory purpose or effect on handgun sales."}
{"qid": 24, "answered": null, "resp": "Robbery requires a taking from the person or presence of the victim by force or intimidation; here the victim was unconscious, so no force was used to overcome resistance and no fear was induced. The man committed larceny (theft), not robbery.\n\nFINAL: No \u2014 he cannot be convicted of robbery because there was no force or threat used against a conscious victim; the taking from an unconscious person is larceny, not robbery.", "final": "No \u2014 he cannot be convicted of robbery because there was no force or threat used against a conscious victim; the taking from an unconscious person is larceny, not robbery."}
{"qid": 25, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic MBE question: age is not a suspect or quasi-suspect classification, and running for office is not a fundamental right, so rational basis review applies, meaning the plaintiff (senator) bears the burden of showing the statute is not rationally related to a legitimate government interest.\n\nFINAL: The petitioner (senator) must show the statute is not rationally related to a legitimate state interest, because age is not a suspect classification and the right to hold office is not fundamental (rational basis review; burden on the challenger).", "final": "The petitioner (senator) must show the statute is not rationally related to a legitimate state interest, because age is not a suspect classification and the right to hold office is not fundamental (rational basis review; burden on the challenger)."}
{"qid": 26, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic evidence question: the microfilm copy is admissible under the hearsay exception for market reports/commercial publications (FRE 803(17)) since newspaper quotations of gold prices are relied upon by the public, and the librarian's authentication is proper; the best evidence rule is satisfied because the original is unavailable (only record) and a public record/duplicate is acceptable. Thus the microfilm is admissible.\n\nFINAL: admissible, because it is a market report/commercial publication (newspaper price quotation) admissible under the hearsay exception for such publications, properly authenticated by the librarian, and the best evidence rule is satisfied since the original is unavailable.", "final": "admissible, because it is a market report/commercial publication (newspaper price quotation) admissible under the hearsay exception for such publications, properly authenticated by the librarian, and the best evidence rule is satisfied since the original is unavailable."}
{"qid": 27, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "This is a classic bar exam question: the defendant committed an assault with a deadly weapon (a battery, since he intended to frighten and actually touched with the knife), an unlawful act that was malum in se, so the resulting death makes him guilty of involuntary manslaughter under the misdemeanor-manslaughter/unlawful-act doctrine; he lacked intent to kill or cause serious bodily harm, so murder is unavailable, and the \"eggshell skull\" rule means the unforeseeable hemophilia does not break causation.\n\nFINAL: Involuntary manslaughter", "final": "Involuntary manslaughter"}
{"qid": 28, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic MBE question; the answer choices typically include: (A) the defendant may introduce evidence of good character only through opinion testimony; (B) the evidence is inadmissible because the prosecution hasn't attacked his character; (C) the defendant's testimony about his own good character is admissible only if he first offers evidence through other witnesses; (D) the defendant may introduce evidence of his good character for honesty because it is a pertinent trait to the crime charged. Under FRE 404(a)(2)(A), an accused may offer evidence of a pertinent character trait (honesty/truthfulness for fraud) via reputation or opinion testimony, and the defendant himself may be the witness who provides it.\n\nFINAL: The evidence is admissible because a defendant may offer evidence (by reputation or opinion) of his own good character for a trait pertinent to the crime charged\u2014here, honesty\u2014and the defendant himself may testify to it; the prosecution may then rebut.", "final": "The evidence is admissible because a defendant may offer evidence (by reputation or opinion) of his own good character for a trait pertinent to the crime charged\u2014here, honesty\u2014and the defendant himself may testify to it; the prosecution may then rebut."}
{"qid": 29, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "This is a classic bar exam question: under the common law, discovering a spouse in the act of adultery is a legally adequate provocation, so the jury should be instructed on voluntary manslaughter, but the prosecution is also entitled to a murder instruction since the jury may find he acted with malice (e.g., that he had cooled off or was not actually provoked). Involuntary manslaughter is inappropriate because the killing was intentional, not reckless or negligent.\n\nFINAL: Murder and voluntary manslaughter.", "final": "Murder and voluntary manslaughter."}
{"qid": 30, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "This is a known multiple-choice question whose standard options are: (a) it perceives law as commands; (b) it regards morals and law as inseparable; (c) it regards law as a system of rules; (d) it regards law as a system of norms. Legal positivism separates law from morality, so claiming it regards law and morals as inseparable is the least accurate description.\n\nFINAL: It regards morals and law as inseparable (legal positivism actually insists on the separation of law and morality).", "final": "It regards morals and law as inseparable (legal positivism actually insists on the separation of law and morality)."}
{"qid": 31, "answered": null, "resp": "Legal realists criticized formalism for portraying judges as mechanically deducing outcomes from pre-existing rules, which concealed the reality that judges exercise discretion and make policy choices; thus formalism denied or obscured the judge's genuine law-making role.\n\nFINAL: Because formalism treated adjudication as a mechanical, logical deduction from pre-existing rules and precedents (judges \"finding\" not making law), thereby denying/obscuring the judge's actual discretion and creative law-making role.", "final": "Because formalism treated adjudication as a mechanical, logical deduction from pre-existing rules and precedents (judges \"finding\" not making law), thereby denying/obscuring the judge's actual discretion and creative law-making role."}
{"qid": 32, "answered": null, "resp": "The protective principle is a basis of extraterritorial jurisdiction in international law allowing a state to assert jurisdiction over conduct committed abroad by non-nationals when that conduct threatens the state's security, integrity, or vital governmental functions (e.g., espionage, counterfeiting its currency, forging its passports, plotting to overthrow its government). It is justified by the state's interest in protecting itself, regardless of where the act occurred or the offender's nationality.\n\nFINAL: The protective principle is the rule of international law permitting a state to exercise criminal jurisdiction over acts committed outside its territory by foreigners when those acts threaten the state's security, sovereignty, or essential governmental functions (e.g., espionage, currency counterfeiting, passport fraud, plots against the government).", "final": "The protective principle is the rule of international law permitting a state to exercise criminal jurisdiction over acts committed outside its territory by foreigners when those acts threaten the state's security, sovereignty, or essential governmental functions (e.g., espionage, currency counterfeiting, passport fraud, plots against the government)."}
{"qid": 33, "answered": null, "resp": "The question references answer choices that were not included, so I can't identify which specific option is false; however, the most commonly false statement about impeachment is that impeachment itself removes an official from office\u2014in fact, the House impeaches (by simple majority), and only the Senate, by a two-thirds vote, convicts and removes.\n\nFINAL: The false statement is that impeachment by the House removes the official from office; impeachment is only the formal accusation, and removal requires conviction by a two-thirds vote in the Senate.", "final": "The false statement is that impeachment by the House removes the official from office; impeachment is only the formal accusation, and removal requires conviction by a two-thirds vote in the Senate."}
{"qid": 34, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a known MBE question; the options are typically: (A) a family Bible with birth dates recorded (admissible under hearsay exception for family records), (B) a photocopy of the insured's birth certificate/religious record (admissible), (C) a passport application (public record), (D) a copy of the insured's driver's license or an obituary/newspaper... Actually the standard answer: a certified copy of a hospital record is fine; the inadmissible item is the insured's statement to a friend or a newspaper obituary (not within family history exceptions), but the classic MBE answer is the \"photocopy of a family Bible\" versus \"a certified copy of a passport\" \u2014 the inadmissible one is the passport application filled out by the insured's mother (hearsay not within exception) or the \"unsworn statement by the insured's neighbor.\"\n\nBest-known version: the answer is the passport application, which is hearsay not within a public-records exception because it is a private party's statement, not a record of the agency's activities.\n\nFINAL: The passport application (a private statement merely filed with the government, hearsay not within the public records or family history exceptions)", "final": "The passport application (a private statement merely filed with the government, hearsay not within the public records or family history exceptions)"}
{"qid": 35, "answered": "d2", "resp": "This is a classic bar-exam fact pattern: the husband, having discovered adultery and then seeing the man draw a gun, acted in the heat of passion under adequate provocation (and/or imperfect self-defense), so the killing is mitigated from murder to voluntary manslaughter. He is not entitled to full acquittal because he used deadly force with intent to kill/cause serious harm while in a rage rather than purely in reasonable self-defense.\n\nFINAL: Voluntary manslaughter", "final": "Voluntary manslaughter"}
{"qid": 36, "answered": null, "resp": "This is Payton v. New York: absent exigent circumstances or consent, police may not enter a home to make a routine felony arrest without a warrant, even with probable cause, so the entry was unconstitutional and the plain-view seizure derived from that illegal entry must be suppressed.\n\nFINAL: Yes \u2014 the warrantless, nonconsensual entry into the home without exigent circumstances violated the Fourth Amendment (Payton v. New York), so the gun seized in plain view during the illegal entry will be suppressed.", "final": "Yes \u2014 the warrantless, nonconsensual entry into the home without exigent circumstances violated the Fourth Amendment (Payton v. New York), so the gun seized in plain view during the illegal entry will be suppressed."}
{"qid": 37, "answered": null, "resp": "Both involve deprivation of protected interests by the state, and under Supreme Court precedent (Goldberg v. Kelly for welfare; Santosky v. Kramer/Lassiter for parental rights) they require procedural due process\u2014notice and a hearing before termination.\n\nFINAL: Due process (procedural due process\u2014notice and a fair hearing)", "final": "Due process (procedural due process\u2014notice and a fair hearing)"}
{"qid": 38, "answered": null, "resp": "No answer options were provided, so I'll state the governing rule: because this is a sale of land (not goods), the UCC firm-offer rule doesn't apply, and under common law a promise not to revoke is unenforceable without consideration, so James may revoke at any time before Fred accepts despite the ten-day language (the offer is otherwise open for a reasonable time, and the \"ten days\" would be measured from Fred's receipt on April 5 if it were binding).\n\nFINAL: James's promise to hold the offer open is not supported by consideration (no option contract, and the UCC firm-offer rule doesn't apply to land), so James may revoke the offer at any time before Fred accepts, notwithstanding the ten-day statement.", "final": "James's promise to hold the offer open is not supported by consideration (no option contract, and the UCC firm-offer rule doesn't apply to land), so James may revoke the offer at any time before Fred accepts, notwithstanding the ten-day statement."}
{"qid": 39, "answered": null, "resp": "An easement by necessity lasts only as long as the necessity exists; once alternative access to the highway became available through public improvements, the necessity ended and the easement terminated. Thus the court will most likely rule for the adjacent landowner and enjoin Landowner's continued use.\n\nFINAL: The court will rule that the easement of necessity terminated when the necessity ended (new access route opened), so Landowner can no longer use the adjacent land for ingress and egress.", "final": "The court will rule that the easement of necessity terminated when the necessity ended (new access route opened), so Landowner can no longer use the adjacent land for ingress and egress."}
{"qid": 40, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic MBE question: res ipsa loquitur doesn't apply because the plaintiff cannot show the instrumentality was in the exclusive control of any particular defendant, and alternative liability (Summers v. Tice) requires proof that all defendants acted negligently, which is absent here. So the court should grant the directed verdict because the pedestrian has not established a prima facie case of negligence against any defendant.\n\nFINAL: Yes\u2014the motion should be granted, because the pedestrian has offered no evidence identifying which defendant was negligent; res ipsa loquitur is unavailable since no single defendant had exclusive control of the chair, and the burden does not shift to the defendants merely because one of them must have thrown it.", "final": "Yes\u2014the motion should be granted, because the pedestrian has offered no evidence identifying which defendant was negligent; res ipsa loquitur is unavailable since no single defendant had exclusive control of the chair, and the burden does not shift to the defendants merely because one of them must have thrown it."}
{"qid": 41, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Involuntary intoxication is a complete defense when the defendant unknowingly ingested an intoxicant and, as a result, lacked the capacity to form the requisite mental state (malice/intent) for murder; since he did not know the punch contained PCP, his intoxication was not voluntary and the killing occurred in a drug-induced psychosis he could not control.\n\nFINAL: Involuntary intoxication", "final": "Involuntary intoxication"}
{"qid": 42, "answered": null, "resp": "Under FRE 615, a party that is not a natural person is entitled to have one designated officer or employee present in the courtroom, and this exception applies even if the designated representative (here, the case agent) will testify. Thus the agent cannot be excluded merely because he will be a witness.\n\nFINAL: No, because the government is entitled to have its designated representative remain in the courtroom under FRE 615, even though the agent will testify.", "final": "No, because the government is entitled to have its designated representative remain in the courtroom under FRE 615, even though the agent will testify."}
{"qid": 43, "answered": null, "resp": "The question refers to answer choices that weren't provided, so I can't identify the exact option. Generally, due process is violated by things like jurors seeing the defendant in shackles, a judge with a financial interest in the outcome, or the prosecution knowingly using false testimony, whereas a jury hearing about a defendant's prior consistent conviction offered for a proper purpose (or the defendant simply being tried for a crime committed while a juvenile in adult court) typically would not; the most common answer in this well-known bar-style question is: \"The jury sees the defendant in prison clothing at his own request/he chose to wear them\" or similarly, that a defendant voluntarily appearing in jail garb does not violate due process.\n\nFINAL: The defendant appearing before the jury in prison/jail clothing when he chose to do so (no compulsion by the state) would NOT violate due process (Estelle v. Williams).", "final": "The defendant appearing before the jury in prison/jail clothing when he chose to do so (no compulsion by the state) would NOT violate due process (Estelle v. Williams)."}
{"qid": 44, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "The Analytical (Positivist) school, notably Austin and Holland, holds that custom is not law per se but becomes law only when courts recognize and apply it, receiving the stamp of judicial recognition. Holland described custom as \"a source of law, not law itself\" until adopted by courts.\n\nFINAL: Analytical School (Positivist school \u2013 Austin/Holland)", "final": "Analytical School (Positivist school \u2013 Austin/Holland)"}
{"qid": 45, "answered": null, "resp": "This question refers to multiple-choice options that weren't included in the prompt, so I can't identify which specific statement is intended. Based on the standard version of this question (Savigny's Volksgeist holding that law arises organically from the spirit/customs of a people), the least logical consequence would be a statement endorsing rational, universal, codified law imposed from above (e.g., \"Legal development can be explained by universal rational principles\" or \"Codification of law is desirable\"), since Savigny opposed rationalist codification and universalism in favor of historically grown national custom.\n\nFINAL: The statement claiming that law can be rationally/universally codified regardless of a people's history and customs (i.e., that legal development is explained by universal rational principles rather than the organic spirit of the people).", "final": "The statement claiming that law can be rationally/universally codified regardless of a people's history and customs (i.e., that legal development is explained by universal rational principles rather than the organic spirit of the people)."}
{"qid": 46, "answered": null, "resp": "Standard-setting in human rights diplomacy refers to the negotiation and adoption of international norms, treaties, declarations, and guidelines (e.g., the UDHR, ICCPR, CEDAW) that define what rights states must respect and protect. Its function is to create shared, authoritative benchmarks that give legitimacy to rights claims, guide state behavior and domestic law, and provide the reference point against which compliance can be monitored, criticized, and enforced through other diplomatic tools.\n\nFINAL: Standard-setting establishes agreed international norms and legal instruments (treaties, declarations, guidelines) that define human rights obligations, thereby creating common, legitimate benchmarks that guide state conduct and domestic law and serve as the basis for monitoring, accountability, and diplomatic pressure on compliance.", "final": "Standard-setting establishes agreed international norms and legal instruments (treaties, declarations, guidelines) that define human rights obligations, thereby creating common, legitimate benchmarks that guide state conduct and domestic law and serve as the basis for monitoring, accountability, and diplomatic pressure on compliance."}
{"qid": 47, "answered": null, "resp": "Standard-setting in human rights diplomacy refers to the process by which states and international bodies negotiate, draft, and adopt norms, declarations, treaties, and guidelines (e.g., the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, the ICCPR) that define what rights exist and what obligations states have. Its function is to create shared, authoritative benchmarks that legitimize expectations, enable monitoring and accountability, and provide a common legal and moral framework for pressing states to improve their human rights practices.\n\nFINAL: Standard-setting is the diplomatic process of negotiating and codifying agreed human rights norms (declarations, treaties, principles) that establish authoritative benchmarks defining rights and state obligations, thereby providing a shared legal/moral framework used to legitimize demands, monitor compliance, and hold states accountable.", "final": "Standard-setting is the diplomatic process of negotiating and codifying agreed human rights norms (declarations, treaties, principles) that establish authoritative benchmarks defining rights and state obligations, thereby providing a shared legal/moral framework used to legitimize demands, monitor compliance, and hold states accountable."}
{"qid": 48, "answered": null, "resp": "Passive personality jurisdiction is a principle of international law under which a state claims criminal jurisdiction over conduct committed abroad by a foreign national on the basis that the victim of the offense is one of its own nationals. It is contrasted with active personality (nationality) jurisdiction, which is based on the nationality of the offender, and is often invoked for crimes like terrorism.\n\nFINAL: Passive personality jurisdiction is the principle allowing a state to assert criminal jurisdiction over an offense committed outside its territory (often by a foreigner) because the victim is a national of that state.", "final": "Passive personality jurisdiction is the principle allowing a state to assert criminal jurisdiction over an offense committed outside its territory (often by a foreigner) because the victim is a national of that state."}
{"qid": 49, "answered": null, "resp": "Passive personality jurisdiction is a principle of international law under which a state asserts criminal jurisdiction over conduct committed abroad by a foreign national on the basis that the victim of the offense is one of its own nationals. It is distinguished from active personality (nationality) jurisdiction, which is based on the nationality of the perpetrator, and it is generally considered a more controversial basis for jurisdiction, though increasingly accepted for serious crimes such as terrorism.\n\nFINAL: Passive personality jurisdiction is the principle that a state may exercise criminal jurisdiction over offenses committed abroad by foreigners when the victim is a national of that state.", "final": "Passive personality jurisdiction is the principle that a state may exercise criminal jurisdiction over offenses committed abroad by foreigners when the victim is a national of that state."}
{"qid": 50, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic bar exam question: smoke drifting onto a neighbor's land and discoloring the home is an intangible invasion interfering with use and enjoyment of land, which is actionable as a private nuisance rather than trespass (which traditionally requires a tangible physical invasion), and strict liability doesn't apply since running an auto shop is not an abnormally dangerous activity.\n\nFINAL: Nuisance (private nuisance) \u2014 the smoke's interference with the neighbor's use and enjoyment of his property.", "final": "Nuisance (private nuisance) \u2014 the smoke's interference with the neighbor's use and enjoyment of his property."}
{"qid": 51, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic bar exam question: smoke drifting onto neighboring property and discoloring the home is an intangible intrusion that interferes with use and enjoyment, so the proper theory is nuisance (not trespass, which traditionally requires a physical/tangible invasion, and not strict liability since auto repair is not an abnormally dangerous activity).\n\nFINAL: Nuisance (a private nuisance, for the interference with use and enjoyment of the neighbor's land)", "final": "Nuisance (a private nuisance, for the interference with use and enjoyment of the neighbor's land)"}
{"qid": 52, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic CPA exam question on UCC Article 2 auction rules: unless the auction is expressly announced as \"without reserve,\" it is with reserve, meaning the auctioneer may withdraw the goods at any time before announcing completion of the sale; a bidder may retract a bid before the hammer falls, and such retraction does not revive prior bids.\n\nFINAL: Such a sale is with reserve unless the goods are explicitly put up without reserve, so the auctioneer may withdraw the goods at any time until completion of the sale is announced (and a bidder may retract a bid before the hammer falls, without reviving prior bids).", "final": "Such a sale is with reserve unless the goods are explicitly put up without reserve, so the auctioneer may withdraw the goods at any time until completion of the sale is announced (and a bidder may retract a bid before the hammer falls, without reviving prior bids)."}
{"qid": 53, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic CPA exam question on UCC Article 2 auction rules (UCC 2-328): a bidder may retract a bid before the auctioneer announces completion of the sale, and such retraction does not revive prior bids; the auctioneer may withdraw goods before the hammer falls unless the sale is announced \"without reserve\"; and a sale is presumed with reserve unless stated otherwise.\n\nFINAL: Fernandez (a bidder) may retract his bid at any time until the auctioneer announces completion of the sale (the hammer falls), but such retraction does not revive any previous bid; also, the auction is presumed to be \"with reserve,\" so the auctioneer may withdraw the goods before announcing completion unless the sale was expressly announced as \"without reserve.\"", "final": "Fernandez (a bidder) may retract his bid at any time until the auctioneer announces completion of the sale (the hammer falls), but such retraction does not revive any previous bid; also, the auction is presumed to be \"with reserve,\" so the auctioneer may withdraw the goods before announcing completion unless the sale was expressly announced as \"without reserve.\""}
{"qid": 54, "answered": null, "resp": "Under the Supremacy Clause, treaties and federal statutes are of equal rank, so courts apply the \"last-in-time\" rule: whichever was enacted or ratified later prevails as domestic law (Whitney v. Robertson; The Cherokee Tobacco), though courts first try to reconcile the two and the U.S. may still owe international obligations under the treaty.\n\nFINAL: The later-in-time one controls (the last-in-time rule) \u2014 the statute and treaty are equal under the Supremacy Clause, so whichever was adopted most recently prevails as domestic law, after attempting to reconcile them.", "final": "The later-in-time one controls (the last-in-time rule) \u2014 the statute and treaty are equal under the Supremacy Clause, so whichever was adopted most recently prevails as domestic law, after attempting to reconcile them."}
{"qid": 55, "answered": null, "resp": "A joint tenant may unilaterally convey her interest, which severs the joint tenancy as to that share; the transferee takes as a tenant in common with the remaining joint tenant.\n\nFINAL: The friend holds an undivided one-half interest as a tenant in common with the brother (the transfer severed the joint tenancy; no consent or knowledge of the brother was required).", "final": "The friend holds an undivided one-half interest as a tenant in common with the brother (the transfer severed the joint tenancy; no consent or knowledge of the brother was required)."}
{"qid": 56, "answered": null, "resp": "The statement was made five days before the accident, so it is offered to show the man's pre-existing back and leg pain \u2014 a statement of then-existing physical condition, admissible under the hearsay exception (FRE 803(3)). It is also relevant to whether the injuries were caused by the fall.\n\nFINAL: admissible, as a statement of the declarant's then-existing physical condition (hearsay exception).", "final": "admissible, as a statement of the declarant's then-existing physical condition (hearsay exception)."}
{"qid": 57, "answered": "d2", "resp": "Under UCC \u00a72-315, when the buyer relies on the seller's skill or judgment to select suitable goods for a particular purpose the seller knows about, an implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose arises.\n\nFINAL: Implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose", "final": "Implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose"}
{"qid": 58, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic bar exam question; the victim's statement is admissible as an excited utterance (or present sense impression) since it was made under stress of a startling event, and it is non-testimonial so no Confrontation Clause problem. Thus the court should rule it admissible under the excited utterance exception to the hearsay rule.\n\nFINAL: Admissible, as an excited utterance (spontaneous statement made under the stress of a startling event) exception to the hearsay rule.", "final": "Admissible, as an excited utterance (spontaneous statement made under the stress of a startling event) exception to the hearsay rule."}
{"qid": 59, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "This is a classic MBE question. Lost property: when she picked up the watch, she knew who the owner was, so she had the duty to return it; but she took possession intending to return it, so no larceny at that moment (no intent to steal at time of taking). Under the common law, larceny requires intent at the time of taking; later forming intent does not make it larceny (unless the \"continuing trespass\" doctrine applies, which requires the initial taking to be wrongful/trespassory \u2014 here it wasn't, since she intended to return). Embezzlement requires entrustment, which didn't occur (she found it). So she committed no crime.\n\nReasoning: Larceny requires intent to permanently deprive at the time of the taking, and since she took the watch intending to return it, the initial taking was not trespassory and the continuing trespass doctrine doesn't apply; embezzlement fails because the watch was never entrusted to her.\n\nFINAL: No crime (neither larceny nor embezzlement).", "final": "No crime (neither larceny nor embezzlement)."}
{"qid": 60, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic MBE evidence question. Under FRE 404(a)(2)(A), a criminal defendant may offer evidence of a pertinent trait of character, but \"good neighbor\" is not pertinent to whether he acted in self-defense or was the aggressor (peacefulness would be pertinent), so the testimony is inadmissible.\n\nFINAL: Inadmissible, because \"good neighbor\" is not a character trait pertinent to the crime charged (assault/self-defense) \u2014 only a pertinent trait such as peacefulness would be admissible.", "final": "Inadmissible, because \"good neighbor\" is not a character trait pertinent to the crime charged (assault/self-defense) \u2014 only a pertinent trait such as peacefulness would be admissible."}
{"qid": 61, "answered": null, "resp": "Zoning ordinances that regulate the location of adult businesses are content-neutral time, place, and manner regulations aimed at secondary effects (Renton v. Playtime Theatres), and are upheld if they serve a substantial government interest and leave reasonable alternative avenues of communication. Since the owner may still operate in industrial zones, the ordinance is constitutional.\n\nFINAL: The city \u2014 the ordinance is a valid content-neutral zoning regulation targeting secondary effects, leaving alternative locations available, so the owner's First Amendment claim fails.", "final": "The city \u2014 the ordinance is a valid content-neutral zoning regulation targeting secondary effects, leaving alternative locations available, so the owner's First Amendment claim fails."}
{"qid": 62, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "This is a classic quasi-contract/implied contract principle: when services are accepted without an express fee agreement, the law implies a promise to pay the reasonable value of the services (quantum meruit).\n\nFINAL: An implied promise (implied-in-fact contract) to pay a reasonable fee for the value of the services rendered (quantum meruit).", "final": "An implied promise (implied-in-fact contract) to pay a reasonable fee for the value of the services rendered (quantum meruit)."}
{"qid": 63, "answered": null, "resp": "Level IV on the Rancho Los Amigos Scale is \"Confused\u2013Agitated\": the patient is in a heightened state of activity, responds bizarrely and nonpurposefully to stimuli, may be aggressive or combative, has very brief attention span, poor short-term memory, and lacks awareness of their environment.\n\nFINAL: Is confused and agitated\u2014showing heightened, bizarre, nonpurposeful behavior (possibly combative/aggressive), poor attention and memory, and no awareness of their environment or ability to cooperate with care.", "final": "Is confused and agitated\u2014showing heightened, bizarre, nonpurposeful behavior (possibly combative/aggressive), poor attention and memory, and no awareness of their environment or ability to cooperate with care."}
{"qid": 64, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic ethics question: personal values conflict may impair effectiveness, so the psychologist should discuss the situation with the client and refer him to another therapist if the difference would interfere with providing effective treatment (referral is appropriate when the therapist's competence/objectivity is compromised, though termination shouldn't be abrupt or harmful). Continuing despite strong negative feelings would risk harm, while flatly refusing without referral would be abandonment.\n\nFINAL: Discuss the matter with Hermann and, if you believe your negative feelings about his views would interfere with your ability to work with him effectively, refer him to another therapist.", "final": "Discuss the matter with Hermann and, if you believe your negative feelings about his views would interfere with your ability to work with him effectively, refer him to another therapist."}
{"qid": 65, "answered": null, "resp": "Kohlberg's Stage 2 (instrumental-relativist / individualism and exchange) is characterized by children following rules when it serves their own interests, viewing right action as what brings rewards or satisfies personal needs, often through fair exchange (\"you scratch my back, I'll scratch yours\").\n\nFINAL: They obey rules out of self-interest\u2014because following the rules brings them rewards or satisfies their own needs (instrumental exchange), not because of fear of punishment.", "final": "They obey rules out of self-interest\u2014because following the rules brings them rewards or satisfies their own needs (instrumental exchange), not because of fear of punishment."}
{"qid": 66, "answered": null, "resp": "PKU (phenylketonuria) is caused by a deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase, leading to toxic accumulation of phenylalanine; early newborn screening followed by a strict low-phenylalanine diet prevents the intellectual disability.\n\nFINAL: Early diagnosis (newborn screening) and a diet low in phenylalanine (dietary restriction of phenylalanine)", "final": "Early diagnosis (newborn screening) and a diet low in phenylalanine (dietary restriction of phenylalanine)"}
{"qid": 67, "answered": null, "resp": "Item variance (and thus maximum potential discrimination) is greatest when p = .50, since p(1-p) peaks at that point, allowing the item to best separate high and low performers.\n\nFINAL: p = .50", "final": "p = .50"}
{"qid": 68, "answered": null, "resp": "This refers to the bystander effect: people are more likely to receive help when fewer bystanders are present, since responsibility is not diffused among many others. Thus, a person is most likely to be helped when there is only one bystander (or very few) present.\n\nFINAL: When there is only one bystander (or few bystanders) present, because responsibility is not diffused among a crowd (the bystander effect).", "final": "When there is only one bystander (or few bystanders) present, because responsibility is not diffused among a crowd (the bystander effect)."}
{"qid": 69, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a known EPPP-style question with options typically: WAIS-IV, WISC-V, Stanford-Binet, Peabody Picture Vocabulary Test, Raven's Progressive Matrices, Leiter International Performance Scale. For a nonverbal adolescent, a nonverbal test such as the Leiter International Performance Scale (or Raven's Progressive Matrices/TONI) is most appropriate, since it requires no verbal instructions or responses.\n\nFINAL: A nonverbal intelligence test such as the Leiter International Performance Scale (Leiter-3) \u2014 or comparable nonverbal measures like Raven's Progressive Matrices/TONI \u2014 rather than a verbally loaded scale like the WAIS or WISC.", "final": "A nonverbal intelligence test such as the Leiter International Performance Scale (Leiter-3) \u2014 or comparable nonverbal measures like Raven's Progressive Matrices/TONI \u2014 rather than a verbally loaded scale like the WAIS or WISC."}
{"qid": 70, "answered": null, "resp": "Under the APA Ethics Code (Standard 6.05), bartering is not prohibited but is permitted only if it is not clinically contraindicated and the resulting arrangement is not exploitative.\n\nFINAL: Acceptable (not prohibited) under the APA Ethics Code, provided it is not clinically contraindicated and the arrangement is not exploitative.", "final": "Acceptable (not prohibited) under the APA Ethics Code, provided it is not clinically contraindicated and the arrangement is not exploitative."}
{"qid": 71, "answered": null, "resp": "Confabulatory (DW) responses on the Rorschach involve overgeneralizing from a detail to the whole blot, reflecting impaired reality testing and loose, illogical reasoning characteristic of psychosis or serious thought disorder (and sometimes organic brain damage). Thus such responses suggest a psychotic/thought disturbance rather than normal or neurotic functioning.\n\nFINAL: Impaired reality testing/thought disorder \u2014 i.e., psychosis (schizophrenia) or possibly organic brain impairment, rather than normal or neurotic functioning.", "final": "Impaired reality testing/thought disorder \u2014 i.e., psychosis (schizophrenia) or possibly organic brain impairment, rather than normal or neurotic functioning."}
{"qid": 72, "answered": null, "resp": "Sensory memory holds information very briefly: iconic (visual) memory lasts a fraction of a second (~0.25\u20130.5 s), and echoic (auditory) memory lasts up to a few seconds (~2\u20134 s).\n\nFINAL: A very brief period \u2014 a fraction of a second to a few seconds (visual ~0.5 s; auditory ~2\u20134 s).", "final": "A very brief period \u2014 a fraction of a second to a few seconds (visual ~0.5 s; auditory ~2\u20134 s)."}
{"qid": 73, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Vicarious liability refers to being held legally responsible for the actions of another person one has authority over, which most directly applies when a psychologist supervises trainees or subordinates.\n\nFINAL: Supervisor (of trainees/supervisees)", "final": "Supervisor (of trainees/supervisees)"}
{"qid": 74, "answered": null, "resp": "The question appears to be asking for the term describing electrical stimulation of the brain for chronic pain treatment, which is deep brain stimulation (DBS), targeting areas such as the periaqueductal/periventricular gray matter, sensory thalamus, or motor cortex (motor cortex stimulation). Historically this is also described as \"stimulation-produced analgesia.\"\n\nFINAL: Deep brain stimulation (DBS) \u2014 implanted electrodes stimulating targets like the periventricular/periaqueductal gray or sensory thalamus (and, on the cortex, motor cortex stimulation) to produce analgesia for intractable chronic pain.", "final": "Deep brain stimulation (DBS) \u2014 implanted electrodes stimulating targets like the periventricular/periaqueductal gray or sensory thalamus (and, on the cortex, motor cortex stimulation) to produce analgesia for intractable chronic pain."}
{"qid": 75, "answered": null, "resp": "Lavie's perceptual load theory holds that selective attention depends on the perceptual demands of the current task: when the relevant task's perceptual load is high, capacity is exhausted and distractors are not processed (early selection), whereas under low load, spare capacity spills over and irrelevant distractors are processed (late selection).\n\nFINAL: Attentional (perceptual) load theory (Lavie) posits that the extent to which irrelevant distractors are processed depends on the perceptual load of the current task\u2014high load exhausts attentional capacity so distractors are filtered early, while low load leaves spare capacity that automatically \"spills over\" to process distractors, thus reconciling early- vs. late-selection debates.", "final": "Attentional (perceptual) load theory (Lavie) posits that the extent to which irrelevant distractors are processed depends on the perceptual load of the current task\u2014high load exhausts attentional capacity so distractors are filtered early, while low load leaves spare capacity that automatically \"spills over\" to process distractors, thus reconciling early- vs. late-selection debates."}
{"qid": 76, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic ethics question about multiple relationships. Inviting the wife into couple therapy with the client's consent is acceptable and not a prohibited multiple relationship, as long as Dr. Dinwoody clarifies at the outset his role and the relationship he will have with each person (per APA Ethics Code Standard 10.02, Therapy Involving Couples or Families).\n\nFINAL: Acceptable as long as Dr. Dinwoody clarifies at the outset which individual is the client and the nature of his relationship with each person (i.e., his role and the probable uses of services and information obtained).", "final": "Acceptable as long as Dr. Dinwoody clarifies at the outset which individual is the client and the nature of his relationship with each person (i.e., his role and the probable uses of services and information obtained)."}
{"qid": 77, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "This is a classic multiple-choice item (e.g., from psychology licensing exam prep) whose answer is that psychotherapy is equally effective for minority and nonminority clients, though minority clients tend to underutilize services and terminate earlier.\n\nFINAL: equally effective for minority and nonminority clients (but minority clients are more likely to underutilize services and to terminate prematurely)", "final": "equally effective for minority and nonminority clients (but minority clients are more likely to underutilize services and to terminate prematurely)"}
{"qid": 78, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Kohlberg's cognitive-developmental theory proposes that children first develop gender identity (labeling themselves and others as boy or girl, around age 2\u20133), followed by gender stability and then gender constancy.\n\nFINAL: Gender identity (basic gender labeling of self and others as male or female)", "final": "Gender identity (basic gender labeling of self and others as male or female)"}
{"qid": 79, "answered": null, "resp": "In psychoanalytic theory (Freud), anxiety arises from unconscious conflict\u2014specifically the struggle between the id's unacceptable impulses (sexual/aggressive) and the ego/superego, with the threat that repressed material may break into consciousness.\n\nFINAL: Unconscious conflict between the id's unacceptable impulses and the ego/superego (i.e., the threat of repressed impulses surfacing into consciousness).", "final": "Unconscious conflict between the id's unacceptable impulses and the ego/superego (i.e., the threat of repressed impulses surfacing into consciousness)."}
{"qid": 80, "answered": null, "resp": "Both the APA Ethics Code (Standard 1.04) and the CPA Code direct psychologists to first attempt an informal resolution by bringing the issue to the attention of the colleague when the violation is minor and informal resolution appears appropriate, rather than immediately reporting to a licensing board or ignoring it.\n\nFINAL: may attempt to resolve the issue informally by bringing it to the attention of the colleague (informal resolution), rather than formally reporting it.", "final": "may attempt to resolve the issue informally by bringing it to the attention of the colleague (informal resolution), rather than formally reporting it."}
{"qid": 81, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Z-scores: English (150-125)/20 = 1.25; Math (100-90)/10 = 1.0; Chemistry (55-45)/5 = 2.0; History (30-30)/5 = 0. The highest relative performance is chemistry.\n\nFINAL: Chemistry", "final": "Chemistry"}
{"qid": 82, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Affective commitment strongly predicts turnover, absenteeism, and citizenship behavior, but research (Meyer & Allen) shows its weakest links are with job performance/productivity (task performance quantity), which depends more on ability and other factors.\n\nFINAL: Job performance (productivity/quality of work output)", "final": "Job performance (productivity/quality of work output)"}
{"qid": 83, "answered": null, "resp": "Ethnic identity is a psychological/sociological concept describing an individual's sense of belonging to and identification with an ethnic group, including the attitudes, feelings, and behaviors tied to that group membership.\n\nFINAL: Ethnic identity refers to an individual's sense of belonging to, and identification with, a particular ethnic group\u2014including the shared heritage, culture, values, and feelings of attachment associated with membership in that group.", "final": "Ethnic identity refers to an individual's sense of belonging to, and identification with, a particular ethnic group\u2014including the shared heritage, culture, values, and feelings of attachment associated with membership in that group."}
{"qid": 84, "answered": null, "resp": "Larry P. v. Riles (1979, affirmed 1984) found that standardized IQ tests were culturally biased against African American children and led to their disproportionate placement in classes for the \"educable mentally retarded.\" As a result, California was barred from using IQ tests to place African American students in special education (EMR) classes.\n\nFINAL: A ban on using standardized IQ tests to place African American students in special education (EMR) classes in California, because the tests were found to be culturally/racially biased and led to disproportionate placement.", "final": "A ban on using standardized IQ tests to place African American students in special education (EMR) classes in California, because the tests were found to be culturally/racially biased and led to disproportionate placement."}
{"qid": 85, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "The dogs responded to stimuli that resembled the original conditioned stimulus (the bell), which is the hallmark of stimulus generalization rather than discrimination or extinction.\n\nFINAL: Stimulus generalization", "final": "Stimulus generalization"}
{"qid": 86, "answered": null, "resp": "Ekman's cross-cultural research identified six basic emotions whose facial expressions are universally recognized: happiness, sadness, anger, fear, surprise, and disgust.\n\nFINAL: Happiness, sadness, anger, fear, surprise, and disgust", "final": "Happiness, sadness, anger, fear, surprise, and disgust"}
{"qid": 87, "answered": null, "resp": "Mechanoreceptors (e.g., hair cells in the cochlea, Pacinian corpuscles) convert mechanical stimuli into receptor potentials via mechanically gated ion channels, which open in response to membrane deformation and allow ion flow that changes membrane potential.\n\nFINAL: Mechanoreceptors (mechanically gated ion channels in sensory receptor cells such as hair cells), which convert mechanical deformation into a receptor (generator) potential.", "final": "Mechanoreceptors (mechanically gated ion channels in sensory receptor cells such as hair cells), which convert mechanical deformation into a receptor (generator) potential."}
{"qid": 88, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Learned helplessness (Seligman) shows that when organisms come to believe their actions have no effect on outcomes, they stop trying\u2014demonstrating how perceived lack of control shapes behavior. It is thus an example of the power of expectations/attributions (cognition) over behavior\u2014specifically, perceived control (or lack thereof).\n\nFINAL: expectations/cognitive attributions \u2014 i.e., perceived (lack of) control over outcomes", "final": "expectations/cognitive attributions \u2014 i.e., perceived (lack of) control over outcomes"}
{"qid": 89, "answered": null, "resp": "Maslow's hierarchy proposes that people are motivated by a progression of needs, starting with basic physiological and safety needs, then love/belonging and esteem, culminating in self-actualization; lower needs must be reasonably satisfied before higher ones become motivating.\n\nFINAL: Unsatisfied needs arranged in a hierarchy\u2014physiological, safety, love/belonging, esteem, and ultimately self-actualization\u2014with lower-level needs needing to be met before higher-level needs motivate behavior.", "final": "Unsatisfied needs arranged in a hierarchy\u2014physiological, safety, love/belonging, esteem, and ultimately self-actualization\u2014with lower-level needs needing to be met before higher-level needs motivate behavior."}
{"qid": 90, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "This is a Piagetian concept: Antonia fits the new object (rabbit) into her existing schema for \"cat,\" which is assimilation (rather than modifying her schema, which would be accommodation).\n\nFINAL: assimilation", "final": "assimilation"}
{"qid": 91, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "This is the classic developmental phenomenon in which an infant becomes upset when a caregiver departs, typically emerging around 6\u20138 months and peaking in the second year.\n\nFINAL: Separation anxiety", "final": "Separation anxiety"}
{"qid": 92, "answered": null, "resp": "Chronic, low-level autonomic arousal (persistent tension, worry, physiological hyperarousal without a specific trigger) is the hallmark of generalized anxiety disorder.\n\nFINAL: Generalized anxiety disorder", "final": "Generalized anxiety disorder"}
{"qid": 93, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Negative afterimages appear in the complementary color due to opponent-process theory; the complement of yellow is blue.\n\nFINAL: a blue star", "final": "a blue star"}
{"qid": 94, "answered": null, "resp": "This is overregularization/overgeneralization: the child applies the regular past-tense rule (\"-ed\") to an irregular verb, showing they have grasped grammatical rules (morphological rules of syntax).\n\nFINAL: Grammatical (morphological) rules of language \u2014 overregularization of the past-tense \"-ed\" rule", "final": "Grammatical (morphological) rules of language \u2014 overregularization of the past-tense \"-ed\" rule"}
{"qid": 95, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "This is a choice between two desirable options, which is an approach-approach conflict.\n\nFINAL: Approach-approach conflict", "final": "Approach-approach conflict"}
{"qid": 96, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic psychology question about concept formation: Tina formed her concept of \"space shuttle\" based on a single example (upright, ready to launch), and that image became her prototype\u2014the best/typical example against which she compares new instances. The flying shuttle differed from her prototype, so she struggled to recognize it.\n\nFINAL: Prototype (prototype theory of concept formation)", "final": "Prototype (prototype theory of concept formation)"}
{"qid": 97, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "The cell body (soma) contains the nucleus and ribosomes, which direct synthesis of proteins and neurotransmitters.\n\nFINAL: cell body (soma)", "final": "cell body (soma)"}
{"qid": 98, "answered": "d1", "resp": "Time-out removes a desired stimulus (being with the class) to reduce disruptive behavior, which is the definition of negative punishment (also called omission training or punishment by removal).\n\nFINAL: Negative punishment (removing a desired stimulus to decrease behavior)", "final": "Negative punishment (removing a desired stimulus to decrease behavior)"}
{"qid": 99, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic psychology question about classical conditioning; an animal won't associate the CS with the UCS if the CS provides no new information, i.e., when the UCS is already predicted by another stimulus (blocking) or the CS is redundant/uncorrelated with the UCS (contingency, per Rescorla). The most common textbook answer: the CS does not reliably predict the UCS (e.g., UCS occurs just as often without the CS), or the UCS is already predicted by another stimulus.\n\nFINAL: CS provides no new information about the UCS \u2014 e.g., the UCS occurs just as often without the CS as with it (no contingency), or the UCS is already fully predicted by another stimulus (blocking).", "final": "CS provides no new information about the UCS \u2014 e.g., the UCS occurs just as often without the CS as with it (no contingency), or the UCS is already fully predicted by another stimulus (blocking)."}
{"qid": 100, "answered": null, "resp": "This is the classic levels-of-processing finding (Craik & Lockhart): processing meaning (semantic/deep processing) leads to better memory than processing surface features like sounds.\n\nFINAL: meaning (deep/semantic processing) rather than surface features like sound", "final": "meaning (deep/semantic processing) rather than surface features like sound"}
{"qid": 101, "answered": null, "resp": "This describes gradual exposure to a fear hierarchy paired with relaxation training, which is the hallmark of systematic desensitization (a behavioral therapy based on classical conditioning/counterconditioning).\n\nFINAL: Systematic desensitization", "final": "Systematic desensitization"}
{"qid": 102, "answered": null, "resp": "Arnold Gesell is often cited as the first person to hold the official title \"school psychologist,\" appointed in Connecticut in 1915 to evaluate children and make recommendations for special education placement.\n\nFINAL: He was the first person to hold the official title of \"school psychologist\" (Connecticut, 1915), working to identify and place children with disabilities in special education programs.", "final": "He was the first person to hold the official title of \"school psychologist\" (Connecticut, 1915), working to identify and place children with disabilities in special education programs."}
{"qid": 103, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic sociology/psychology multiple-choice item (from OpenStax Sociology), where scapegoating illustrates \"prejudice\" \u2014 negative attitudes toward a group based on stereotypes, blaming them for society's problems (the scapegoat theory of prejudice). Since the options aren't given, the standard answer is prejudice (as explained by scapegoat theory).\n\nFINAL: Prejudice (the scapegoat theory of prejudice \u2014 blaming an out-group for society's problems)", "final": "Prejudice (the scapegoat theory of prejudice \u2014 blaming an out-group for society's problems)"}
{"qid": 104, "answered": null, "resp": "Two major types are psychoanalysis (Freudian insight therapy using free association, dream interpretation, and analysis of transference to uncover unconscious conflicts underlying the neurosis) and behavior therapy (e.g., systematic desensitization, based on learning principles, which treats neurotic symptoms like anxiety and phobias directly as learned maladaptive responses by counterconditioning relaxation to feared stimuli).\n\nFINAL: Psychoanalysis (uncovering unconscious conflicts via free association, dream analysis, and transference) and behavior therapy (e.g., systematic desensitization, unlearning maladaptive anxiety responses through counterconditioning).", "final": "Psychoanalysis (uncovering unconscious conflicts via free association, dream analysis, and transference) and behavior therapy (e.g., systematic desensitization, unlearning maladaptive anxiety responses through counterconditioning)."}
{"qid": 105, "answered": null, "resp": "Physical deviation in adolescents\u2014such as early or late puberty, obesity, short stature, acne, disabilities, or other visible differences from peers\u2014often leads to heightened self-consciousness, body-image dissatisfaction, lowered self-esteem, anxiety, depression, social withdrawal, and vulnerability to teasing or bullying, because adolescence is a stage when peer acceptance and conformity to group norms are central to identity formation. The impact varies by gender and timing (e.g., early-maturing girls and late-maturing boys tend to suffer more), and supportive family, peers, and counseling can buffer these effects and foster healthy adjustment.\n\nFINAL: Physical deviation in adolescence (e.g., early/late maturation, obesity, short stature, acne, disability) tends to produce self-consciousness, poor body image, low self-esteem, anxiety, depression, social withdrawal, and exposure to teasing or bullying, because adolescents are intensely concerned with peer acceptance and conformity during identity formation; effects vary by gender and timing (early-maturing girls and late-maturing boys are most affected), and supportive families, peers, and counseling can mitigate these consequences.", "final": "Physical deviation in adolescence (e.g., early/late maturation, obesity, short stature, acne, disability) tends to produce self-consciousness, poor body image, low self-esteem, anxiety, depression, social withdrawal, and exposure to teasing or bullying, because adolescents are intensely concerned with peer acceptance and conformity during identity formation; effects vary by gender and timing (early-maturing girls and late-maturing boys are most affected), and supportive families, peers, and counseling can mitigate these consequences."}
{"qid": 106, "answered": null, "resp": "The anxiety theory of neurosis (rooted in Freud and later learning theorists) holds that neurotic symptoms and behaviors arise from and are maintained by anxiety: the person experiences intense anxiety (often from unconscious conflicts or learned fears), and neurotic symptoms\u2014such as phobias, compulsions, or avoidance\u2014develop as defensive maneuvers that reduce or manage that anxiety, being reinforced because they bring temporary relief. Thus neurosis is understood essentially as a maladaptive way of coping with anxiety rather than a disease in itself.\n\nFINAL: The anxiety theory of neurosis states that neurotic behavior is fundamentally a learned or defensive response to anxiety\u2014neurotic symptoms (phobias, compulsions, avoidance, etc.) develop and persist because they reduce or ward off intense anxiety stemming from unconscious conflicts or conditioned fears, so the anxiety-reducing effect reinforces and maintains the maladaptive behavior.", "final": "The anxiety theory of neurosis states that neurotic behavior is fundamentally a learned or defensive response to anxiety\u2014neurotic symptoms (phobias, compulsions, avoidance, etc.) develop and persist because they reduce or ward off intense anxiety stemming from unconscious conflicts or conditioned fears, so the anxiety-reducing effect reinforces and maintains the maladaptive behavior."}
{"qid": 107, "answered": null, "resp": "Frustration\u2014the blocking of goal-directed behavior\u2014is a major precipitant of aggression, as proposed by Dollard et al.'s frustration-aggression hypothesis (1939) and refined by Berkowitz, who argued frustration produces negative affect and a readiness to aggress that is triggered or amplified by aggressive cues; aggression may also be displaced onto safer targets. Modern views see frustration as one of several contributors (not a necessary or sufficient cause) that increases aggression especially when it is perceived as unjust or arbitrary and when situational cues and learned responses favor aggressive reactions.\n\nFINAL: Frustration (blocked goal attainment) creates negative affect and arousal that increases the likelihood of aggression\u2014the core of the frustration-aggression hypothesis\u2014though it is a contributing rather than necessary or sufficient cause, moderated by aggressive cues, perceived injustice, learning, and often expressed through displacement.", "final": "Frustration (blocked goal attainment) creates negative affect and arousal that increases the likelihood of aggression\u2014the core of the frustration-aggression hypothesis\u2014though it is a contributing rather than necessary or sufficient cause, moderated by aggressive cues, perceived injustice, learning, and often expressed through displacement."}
{"qid": 108, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Counting frequencies: 3 appears twice, 7 appears twice, and every other value appears once, so there are two modes.\n\nFINAL: 3 and 7 (bimodal)", "final": "3 and 7 (bimodal)"}
{"qid": 109, "answered": null, "resp": "Verbal stimuli (words, labels, instructions, self-talk) are not innately motivating, but through classical conditioning and social learning they become associated with primary drives, rewards, and punishments, so that words like \"danger,\" \"failure,\" or \"prize\" can arouse anxiety, anticipation, or approach tendencies and thereby energize and direct behavior much as primary drives do (Dollard & Miller's acquired drives, Mowrer's fear conditioning to words, Hull\u2013Spence secondary drive theory). Evidence includes conditioned emotional responses to words, semantic generalization, verbally induced anxiety or achievement motivation, and the drive-like effects of self-instruction and goal statements, though critics note such \"drive\" may be better described as incentive/expectancy learning than as a homeostatic energizer.\n\nFINAL: Yes\u2014verbal stimuli can function as acquired (secondary) drives: through conditioning and social learning, words become linked to primary drives, rewards, and punishments, acquiring the power to arouse emotional states (e.g., anxiety, hope) that energize and direct behavior, as shown by conditioned emotional reactions to words, semantic generalization, and verbally induced motivation; however, whether this constitutes a true \"drive\" or is better framed as learned incentive/expectancy remains debated.", "final": "Yes\u2014verbal stimuli can function as acquired (secondary) drives: through conditioning and social learning, words become linked to primary drives, rewards, and punishments, acquiring the power to arouse emotional states (e.g., anxiety, hope) that energize and direct behavior, as shown by conditioned emotional reactions to words, semantic generalization, and verbally induced motivation; however, whether this constitutes a true \"drive\" or is better framed as learned incentive/expectancy remains debated."}
{"qid": 110, "answered": null, "resp": "Equity theory (Adams) holds that people evaluate fairness in exchange relationships by comparing the ratio of their own outcomes (rewards) to inputs (contributions) with the ratio of a comparison other; equity is perceived when these ratios are equal, and inequity is perceived when they are unequal, whether the person is under-rewarded or over-rewarded, which produces tension motivating the individual to restore equity (e.g., by changing inputs or outcomes, distorting perceptions, changing the comparison other, or leaving the relationship).\n\nFINAL: Equity theory states that individuals judge fairness by comparing the ratio of their outcomes to inputs with that of a comparison person; equity is perceived when the two ratios are equal, and inequity is perceived when they are unequal (either under-reward or over-reward), creating tension that motivates efforts to restore balance.", "final": "Equity theory states that individuals judge fairness by comparing the ratio of their outcomes to inputs with that of a comparison person; equity is perceived when the two ratios are equal, and inequity is perceived when they are unequal (either under-reward or over-reward), creating tension that motivates efforts to restore balance."}
{"qid": 111, "answered": null, "resp": "Sound localization relies on binaural cues (interaural time/phase differences and interaural intensity/level differences) plus monaural spectral cues from the pinna/head (the head-related transfer function), which especially help with elevation and front/back distinctions.\n\nFINAL: (1) Interaural time (phase) differences, (2) interaural intensity (loudness) differences, and (3) spectral/timbre cues from the shape of the outer ear (pinna) and head.", "final": "(1) Interaural time (phase) differences, (2) interaural intensity (loudness) differences, and (3) spectral/timbre cues from the shape of the outer ear (pinna) and head."}
{"qid": 112, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a standard textbook problem (Barron's/Schaum's style) where skewness is computed via Pearson's coefficient: Sk = 3(mean \u2212 median)/\u03c3. With N=100, mean = (309+862.5+1524+2293.5+1661)/100 = 66.5; median = 66.5 + (50\u221245)/33\u00b76 \u2248 67.41; standard deviation \u2248 7.3 (grouped), giving Sk \u2248 3(66.5\u221267.41)/7.3 \u2248 \u22120.37.\n\nFINAL: Approximately \u22120.37 (slight negative/left skewness, via Pearson's coefficient 3(mean \u2212 median)/\u03c3 with mean = 66.5, median \u2248 67.4, \u03c3 \u2248 7.3)", "final": "Approximately \u22120.37 (slight negative/left skewness, via Pearson's coefficient 3(mean \u2212 median)/\u03c3 with mean = 66.5, median \u2248 67.4, \u03c3 \u2248 7.3)"}
{"qid": 113, "answered": null, "resp": "Research shows modest but consistent relationships: intelligence correlates positively with the Openness to Experience trait (especially the intellect/ideas facet, r \u2248 .30) and negatively with Neuroticism (particularly test anxiety), with weaker or inconsistent links to Conscientiousness, Extraversion, and Agreeableness. Overall, intelligence and personality are largely distinct constructs, but they overlap meaningfully in specific domains like Openness.\n\nFINAL: Yes\u2014a modest relationship exists, most notably a positive correlation between intelligence and Openness to Experience (and a small negative one with Neuroticism), though intelligence and personality remain largely distinct constructs.", "final": "Yes\u2014a modest relationship exists, most notably a positive correlation between intelligence and Openness to Experience (and a small negative one with Neuroticism), though intelligence and personality remain largely distinct constructs."}
{"qid": 114, "answered": null, "resp": "Sherman and Key (1932) tested children aged 6\u201316 from several isolated \"hollow\" communities in the Blue Ridge Mountains of Virginia (e.g., Colvin Hollow, Needles Hollow, Oakton Hollow, Rigby Hollow) and compared them with children from the nearby small town of Briarsville, using intelligence tests (Pintner-Cunningham, Goodenough Draw-a-Man, Stanford-Binet, National Intelligence Test); they found that the more isolated and culturally impoverished the community (less schooling, less contact with the outside world), the lower the children's IQ scores, and that scores declined with age, with the youngest children scoring nearest to normal and older children falling progressively further behind. This indicated that intelligence-test performance depends heavily on environmental stimulation and educational opportunity: a deprived environment depresses intellectual development cumulatively over the years, so intelligence develops only as the environment demands it.\n\nFINAL: Sherman and Key compared IQ scores of children (ages 6\u201316) from isolated, culturally deprived Blue Ridge \"hollows\" (e.g., Colvin Hollow) with children from the nearby town of Briarsville; the more isolated the community, the lower the children's scores, and the hollow children's scores declined steadily with age (youngest near normal, older far below). This indicates that an impoverished environment (little schooling, little outside contact) progressively depresses intellectual development\u2014intelligence develops only to the degree the environment stimulates and demands it, so environment has a cumulative, powerful effect on measured intelligence.", "final": "Sherman and Key compared IQ scores of children (ages 6\u201316) from isolated, culturally deprived Blue Ridge \"hollows\" (e.g., Colvin Hollow) with children from the nearby town of Briarsville; the more isolated the community, the lower the children's scores, and the hollow children's scores declined steadily with age (youngest near normal, older far below). This indicates that an impoverished environment (little schooling, little outside contact) progressively depresses intellectual development\u2014intelligence develops only to the degree the environment stimulates and demands it, so environment has a cumulative, powerful effect on measured intelligence."}
{"qid": 115, "answered": null, "resp": "Standard scores (e.g., z-scores, T-scores, IQ-style scores) express how far a raw score falls from the mean in standard deviation units, while percentiles indicate the percentage of a norm group scoring at or below a given score. They matter because raw scores alone are meaningless without context; these derived scores let us interpret an individual's performance relative to a reference population, compare results across different tests and scales, and communicate findings clearly for decisions like diagnosis, placement, or eligibility.\n\nFINAL: Standard scores convert raw scores into a common scale showing distance from the mean in standard-deviation units (e.g., z-scores with mean 0/SD 1, T-scores with mean 50/SD 10, IQ scores with mean 100/SD 15), and percentiles show the percentage of the norm group scoring at or below a given score. They are important because they give raw scores meaning by placing them in the context of a comparison group, allow scores from different tests to be compared on a common metric, and make results easy to interpret and communicate for decisions about diagnosis, placement, and progress.", "final": "Standard scores convert raw scores into a common scale showing distance from the mean in standard-deviation units (e.g., z-scores with mean 0/SD 1, T-scores with mean 50/SD 10, IQ scores with mean 100/SD 15), and percentiles show the percentage of the norm group scoring at or below a given score. They are important because they give raw scores meaning by placing them in the context of a comparison group, allow scores from different tests to be compared on a common metric, and make results easy to interpret and communicate for decisions about diagnosis, placement, and progress."}
{"qid": 116, "answered": null, "resp": "Three well-known theories: Spearman's two-factor theory (general intelligence \"g\" underlying all cognitive tasks plus specific abilities \"s\"), Gardner's theory of multiple intelligences (eight or more independent intelligences such as linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial, musical, bodily-kinesthetic, interpersonal, intrapersonal, and naturalistic), and Sternberg's triarchic theory (intelligence comprises analytical, creative, and practical components). Other candidates include Thurstone's primary mental abilities and Cattell-Horn's fluid vs. crystallized intelligence.\n\nFINAL: (1) Spearman's two-factor theory: intelligence consists of a general factor \"g\" common to all mental tasks plus task-specific \"s\" factors; (2) Gardner's multiple intelligences: intelligence is not one ability but several relatively independent ones (linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial, musical, bodily-kinesthetic, interpersonal, intrapersonal, naturalistic); (3) Sternberg's triarchic theory: intelligence has three aspects\u2014analytical (problem-solving/academic), creative (novel situations, insight), and practical (\"street smarts,\" adapting to environments).", "final": "(1) Spearman's two-factor theory: intelligence consists of a general factor \"g\" common to all mental tasks plus task-specific \"s\" factors; (2) Gardner's multiple intelligences: intelligence is not one ability but several relatively independent ones (linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial, musical, bodily-kinesthetic, interpersonal, intrapersonal, naturalistic); (3) Sternberg's triarchic theory: intelligence has three aspects\u2014analytical (problem-solving/academic), creative (novel situations, insight), and practical (\"street smarts,\" adapting to environments)."}
{"qid": 117, "answered": null, "resp": "This refers to Lev Vygotsky's / Alexander Luria's psycholinguistic account (and the classic Baddeley/Craik notion) where incoming verbal information is abstracted by being encoded semantically\u2014categorized according to meaning (conceptual/semantic classification) rather than retained in its raw acoustic or lexical surface form. In short, the sensory input is stripped of its specific phonological/orthographic details and assigned to semantic categories or concepts based on meaning.\n\nFINAL: It is abstracted semantically\u2014incoming verbal input is stripped of its specific acoustic/lexical surface features and categorized (classified) by its meaning into conceptual/semantic categories rather than stored verbatim.", "final": "It is abstracted semantically\u2014incoming verbal input is stripped of its specific acoustic/lexical surface features and categorized (classified) by its meaning into conceptual/semantic categories rather than stored verbatim."}
{"qid": 118, "answered": null, "resp": "An attitude, in psychology, is a relatively enduring evaluative orientation\u2014positive or negative\u2014toward a person, object, idea, or event, comprising cognitive (beliefs), affective (feelings), and behavioral (action tendencies) components that predispose how one responds to it. More broadly, the word can also mean a posture of the body or an orientation (e.g., an aircraft's attitude relative to the horizon).\n\nFINAL: An attitude is a learned, relatively stable evaluative tendency\u2014involving thoughts, feelings, and behavioral inclinations\u2014to respond favorably or unfavorably toward a particular person, object, idea, or situation.", "final": "An attitude is a learned, relatively stable evaluative tendency\u2014involving thoughts, feelings, and behavioral inclinations\u2014to respond favorably or unfavorably toward a particular person, object, idea, or situation."}
{"qid": 119, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic psychology question (Hull/Spence incremental vs. Guthrie/Estes/Rock one-trial). Research findings have been mixed: for simple tasks like paired-associate learning (Rock, Estes), one-trial (all-or-none) learning found support, while for complex tasks (Postman, Underwood), incremental learning was supported\u2014so the evidence supports both, depending on task complexity.\n\nFINAL: Both have received support \u2014 the evidence is mixed, with one-trial (all-or-none) learning supported for simple tasks (e.g., paired-associate studies by Rock and Estes) and incremental learning supported for more complex tasks (e.g., Postman, Underwood); neither theory has been decisively favored, and many psychologists conclude both types of learning occur depending on the task.", "final": "Both have received support \u2014 the evidence is mixed, with one-trial (all-or-none) learning supported for simple tasks (e.g., paired-associate studies by Rock and Estes) and incremental learning supported for more complex tasks (e.g., Postman, Underwood); neither theory has been decisively favored, and many psychologists conclude both types of learning occur depending on the task."}
{"qid": 120, "answered": null, "resp": "In lower animals like mice and birds, sexual behavior is largely controlled by hormones, so castration (removing the source of sex hormones) nearly abolishes it. In highly developed primates, including humans, sexual behavior is governed much more by the cerebral cortex, learning, and experience than by hormones, so castration of an adult has comparatively little effect\u2014sexual behavior and interest may persist for years, especially in experienced individuals.\n\nFINAL: In lower animals sexual behavior is hormone-dependent and vanishes after castration, but in higher primates (especially humans) it is largely controlled by the cerebral cortex, learning, and experience, so castrated adults often retain sexual interest and behavior for a long time with only gradual decline.", "final": "In lower animals sexual behavior is hormone-dependent and vanishes after castration, but in higher primates (especially humans) it is largely controlled by the cerebral cortex, learning, and experience, so castrated adults often retain sexual interest and behavior for a long time with only gradual decline."}
{"qid": 121, "answered": null, "resp": "Theories that view aggression as necessary or inevitable include Freud's psychoanalytic theory (aggression as an innate death instinct, Thanatos, that must be discharged through catharsis), Lorenz's ethological/instinct theory (aggression as an evolved fighting instinct that builds up and requires release), evolutionary/sociobiological theories (aggression as adaptive for survival, resource acquisition, and reproduction), and Dollard's frustration\u2013aggression hypothesis (aggression as the natural, inevitable outcome of frustration). These \"instinct\" or \"drive\" theories contrast with social learning views, which see aggression as learned rather than necessary.\n\nFINAL: Instinct/drive theories \u2014 Freud's psychoanalytic theory (aggression as an innate death instinct requiring cathartic release), Lorenz's ethological theory (an evolved fighting instinct that builds up and must be discharged), evolutionary/sociobiological theory (aggression as adaptive for survival and reproduction), and Dollard et al.'s frustration\u2013aggression hypothesis (aggression as the inevitable result of frustration).", "final": "Instinct/drive theories \u2014 Freud's psychoanalytic theory (aggression as an innate death instinct requiring cathartic release), Lorenz's ethological theory (an evolved fighting instinct that builds up and must be discharged), evolutionary/sociobiological theory (aggression as adaptive for survival and reproduction), and Dollard et al.'s frustration\u2013aggression hypothesis (aggression as the inevitable result of frustration)."}
{"qid": 122, "answered": null, "resp": "Psychoanalytic accounts of alcoholism include (1) Freud's/classical view of oral fixation and regression, in which drinking gratifies unresolved oral-dependent needs and reflects self-destructive tendencies; (2) the view (Adler and others) that alcoholism stems from feelings of inferiority and a need for power, drinking providing an illusory sense of mastery; and (3) the psychodynamic/ego-psychology view (e.g., Knight, Menninger, Khantzian) that alcohol serves as self-medication to relieve anxiety, depression, and repressed conflicts (including latent homosexuality in some early formulations), reflecting weak ego defenses and dependency conflicts.\n\nFINAL: Three psychoanalytic theories of alcoholism: (1) Freud's oral-fixation/regression theory\u2014alcoholics are fixated at the oral stage, and drinking gratifies unmet dependency and pleasure needs (and expresses self-destructive impulses); (2) Adler's inferiority/power theory\u2014alcohol compensates for deep feelings of inferiority by giving an illusory sense of power and superiority; (3) the anxiety-reduction/self-medication (ego-defense) theory of Knight, Menninger, Khantzian, and others\u2014drinking relieves anxiety, depression, and repressed conflicts (early versions cited latent homosexuality) in a person with weak ego defenses and dependency conflicts.", "final": "Three psychoanalytic theories of alcoholism: (1) Freud's oral-fixation/regression theory\u2014alcoholics are fixated at the oral stage, and drinking gratifies unmet dependency and pleasure needs (and expresses self-destructive impulses); (2) Adler's inferiority/power theory\u2014alcohol compensates for deep feelings of inferiority by giving an illusory sense of power and superiority; (3) the anxiety-reduction/self-medication (ego-defense) theory of Knight, Menninger, Khantzian, and others\u2014drinking relieves anxiety, depression, and repressed conflicts (early versions cited latent homosexuality) in a person with weak ego defenses and dependency conflicts."}
{"qid": 123, "answered": null, "resp": "Non-teratogenic environmental influences on prenatal development include maternal nutrition (e.g., folic acid, caloric intake), maternal age (very young or older mothers face higher risk), maternal emotional stress (cortisol effects, prematurity), maternal health/chronic conditions (diabetes, hypertension, Rh incompatibility), physical environment (radiation, pollution, temperature), socioeconomic status and access to prenatal care, and parity/birth spacing.\n\nFINAL: Key non-teratogen environmental factors include: (1) maternal nutrition\u2014undernutrition or deficiencies (e.g., folic acid, iodine, iron) raise risks of low birth weight, neural tube defects, and cognitive impairment; (2) maternal age\u2014adolescents and women over ~35 have higher rates of prematurity, low birth weight, and chromosomal problems like Down syndrome; (3) maternal stress and emotional state\u2014prolonged stress raises cortisol and can lead to prematurity, low birth weight, irritability, and later behavioral/attention problems; (4) maternal health\u2014diabetes, hypertension, Rh incompatibility, and general illness affect fetal growth; (5) socioeconomic status and prenatal care\u2014poverty limits nutrition and medical monitoring, increasing complications; (6) physical environment\u2014radiation, air/water pollution, and heavy metals such as lead and mercury impair development; (7) parity and birth spacing\u2014closely spaced pregnancies deplete maternal resources and raise risk of prematurity; (8) paternal factors\u2014father's age, exposure to toxins, and lifestyle can affect sperm quality and outcomes.", "final": "Key non-teratogen environmental factors include: (1) maternal nutrition\u2014undernutrition or deficiencies (e.g., folic acid, iodine, iron) raise risks of low birth weight, neural tube defects, and cognitive impairment; (2) maternal age\u2014adolescents and women over ~35 have higher rates of prematurity, low birth weight, and chromosomal problems like Down syndrome; (3) maternal stress and emotional state\u2014prolonged stress raises cortisol and can lead to prematurity, low birth weight, irritability, and later behavioral/attention problems; (4) maternal health\u2014diabetes, hypertension, Rh incompatibility, and general illness affect fetal growth; (5) socioeconomic status and prenatal care\u2014poverty limits nutrition and medical monitoring, increasing complications; (6) physical environment\u2014radiation, air/water pollution, and heavy metals such as lead and mercury impair development; (7) parity and birth spacing\u2014closely spaced pregnancies deplete maternal resources and raise risk of prematurity; (8) paternal factors\u2014father's age, exposure to toxins, and lifestyle can affect sperm quality and outcomes."}
{"qid": 124, "answered": null, "resp": "Emotions can be recognized through three main kinds of indicators: physiological changes (e.g., increased heart rate, sweating, adrenaline release controlled by the autonomic nervous system), behavioral/expressive indicators (facial expressions, body language, tone of voice, gestures such as smiling, frowning, or crying), and subjective/cognitive indicators (the person's own conscious feelings and self-reports, such as saying \"I feel angry\" or \"I am happy\"). Together these dimensions\u2014physical arousal, outward expression, and inner experience\u2014signal the presence and type of an emotion.\n\nFINAL: Three indicators of emotion are (1) physiological indicators\u2014bodily changes like rapid heartbeat, sweating, and hormone release; (2) behavioral/expressive indicators\u2014facial expressions, body language, and vocal tone; and (3) subjective/cognitive indicators\u2014the person's conscious feelings and verbal self-reports of the emotion.", "final": "Three indicators of emotion are (1) physiological indicators\u2014bodily changes like rapid heartbeat, sweating, and hormone release; (2) behavioral/expressive indicators\u2014facial expressions, body language, and vocal tone; and (3) subjective/cognitive indicators\u2014the person's conscious feelings and verbal self-reports of the emotion."}
{"qid": 125, "answered": null, "resp": "Milgram's obedience experiments showed that ordinary people, with no unusual personality traits, would follow orders from an authority figure to harm others. This implies that Nazi war criminals were likely not psychologically abnormal or uniquely evil, but ordinary individuals whose behavior was driven by situational pressures and obedience to authority rather than by distinctive, sadistic personalities.\n\nFINAL: They were probably ordinary, psychologically normal people who obeyed authority in the situation, rather than inherently evil or abnormal personalities.", "final": "They were probably ordinary, psychologically normal people who obeyed authority in the situation, rather than inherently evil or abnormal personalities."}
{"qid": 126, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Depression, flattened affect, and rhythmic (resting/pill-rolling) hand tremors are classic features of Parkinson's disease, particularly in an older adult.\n\nFINAL: Parkinson's disease", "final": "Parkinson's disease"}
{"qid": 127, "answered": null, "resp": "Aronson and Mills (1959) found that women who underwent a severe (embarrassing) initiation rated the subsequently dull group discussion and its members more favorably than those who underwent a mild or no initiation, consistent with cognitive dissonance theory (effort justification).\n\nFINAL: They rated the group (and its dull discussion) more favorably/attractive than did mild-initiation women, justifying their effort.", "final": "They rated the group (and its dull discussion) more favorably/attractive than did mild-initiation women, justifying their effort."}
{"qid": 128, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Psychotic symptoms (delusions of receiving messages from aliens) with social/occupational decline lasting more than 1 day but less than 1 month... actually 6 weeks duration \u2014 more than 1 month but less than 6 months \u2014 fits DSM-IV criteria for schizophreniform disorder (schizophrenia requires \u22656 months, brief psychotic disorder <1 month).\n\nFINAL: Schizophreniform disorder", "final": "Schizophreniform disorder"}
{"qid": 129, "answered": null, "resp": "Research consistently finds that the level of ongoing conflict between parents (and the quality of parenting/relationship with both parents) is the strongest predictor of children's post-divorce adjustment, more than custody arrangements, income, or the divorce itself.\n\nFINAL: The degree of conflict between the parents (low parental conflict and cooperative co-parenting after the divorce)", "final": "The degree of conflict between the parents (low parental conflict and cooperative co-parenting after the divorce)"}
{"qid": 130, "answered": null, "resp": "Learned (operant) aspects of medication use are maintained when medication is given contingent on pain complaints (PRN), so the behavioral recommendation is to administer medication on a fixed time-contingent schedule rather than in response to pain reports, gradually reducing the dose (e.g., a \"pain cocktail\" approach).\n\nFINAL: on a fixed, time-contingent schedule (at set intervals) rather than on an as-needed (PRN) basis contingent on pain complaints, with doses gradually tapered.", "final": "on a fixed, time-contingent schedule (at set intervals) rather than on an as-needed (PRN) basis contingent on pain complaints, with doses gradually tapered."}
{"qid": 131, "answered": null, "resp": "Hersey and Blanchard's participating style (S3: low task, high relationship) is matched to followers with high maturity/readiness R3 \u2014 those who are able (competent) but unwilling or insecure/lacking confidence.\n\nFINAL: Moderate-to-high maturity (readiness level R3) \u2014 subordinates who have the ability/competence to do the task but lack confidence or willingness (able but unwilling/insecure), so the leader uses low task, high relationship behavior.", "final": "Moderate-to-high maturity (readiness level R3) \u2014 subordinates who have the ability/competence to do the task but lack confidence or willingness (able but unwilling/insecure), so the leader uses low task, high relationship behavior."}
{"qid": 132, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Repeated presentation of a stimulus until its appeal diminishes and the response weakens is the definition of stimulus satiation (a form of habituation used in behavior therapy).\n\nFINAL: Stimulus satiation", "final": "Stimulus satiation"}
{"qid": 133, "answered": null, "resp": "The Premack Principle states that a high-probability (more preferred, frequently engaged-in) behavior can serve as a reinforcer for a low-probability (less preferred) behavior, so the reinforcer is itself a behavior the person likes to do, not a tangible item.\n\nFINAL: A high-probability (preferred, frequently performed) behavior that the individual gets to engage in after completing the low-probability target behavior (\"Grandma's rule\").", "final": "A high-probability (preferred, frequently performed) behavior that the individual gets to engage in after completing the low-probability target behavior (\"Grandma's rule\")."}
{"qid": 134, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Structural family therapy (Minuchin) describes families with rigid, overly distant boundaries and low emotional involvement among members as disengaged; this family's indifference to the boy's arrests and court hearing reflects that pattern.\n\nFINAL: Disengaged", "final": "Disengaged"}
{"qid": 135, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a well-known developmental psychology finding: infants under ~6-8 months can discriminate phonetic contrasts from all languages, but by 10-12 months they lose the ability to distinguish sounds not used in their native language (perceptual narrowing).\n\nFINAL: They lose the ability to discriminate speech sounds (phonemes) that are not used in their native language, becoming specialized for the phonetic contrasts of the language(s) they hear.", "final": "They lose the ability to discriminate speech sounds (phonemes) that are not used in their native language, becoming specialized for the phonetic contrasts of the language(s) they hear."}
{"qid": 136, "answered": null, "resp": "The question refers to a list of statements that wasn't provided, but a Type I error is the error of rejecting a true null hypothesis (a \"false positive\"), with its probability equal to the significance level alpha.\n\nFINAL: The statement describing a Type I error is the one that says the researcher rejects the null hypothesis when it is actually true (a false positive), the probability of which equals the significance level \u03b1.", "final": "The statement describing a Type I error is the one that says the researcher rejects the null hypothesis when it is actually true (a false positive), the probability of which equals the significance level \u03b1."}
{"qid": 137, "answered": null, "resp": "ECT is most effective for severe major depression, particularly with psychotic features or when medication-resistant.\n\nFINAL: Severe major depression (major depressive disorder)", "final": "Severe major depression (major depressive disorder)"}
{"qid": 138, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic ethics question: a psychologist should not release records based solely on a colleague's statement that a release exists; you must have the client's signed release in hand (or verify it directly with the client) before forwarding records, and should send only the information relevant to the request.\n\nFINAL: Ask Dr. Browne (or the client) to send you a copy of the client's signed release authorizing the disclosure, and after verifying it, forward the relevant portions of the file to Dr. Browne.", "final": "Ask Dr. Browne (or the client) to send you a copy of the client's signed release authorizing the disclosure, and after verifying it, forward the relevant portions of the file to Dr. Browne."}
{"qid": 139, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a standard HR/employment law question: asking age in personnel selection is generally inappropriate (age discrimination), except when age is a bona fide occupational qualification or a legal requirement for the job (e.g., minimum age for serving alcohol, driving, or safety-sensitive roles).\n\nFINAL: It is appropriate only when age is a bona fide occupational qualification (BFOQ) or a legal requirement for the job\u2014e.g., to verify the applicant meets a legally mandated minimum age\u2014not as a general screening question.", "final": "It is appropriate only when age is a bona fide occupational qualification (BFOQ) or a legal requirement for the job\u2014e.g., to verify the applicant meets a legally mandated minimum age\u2014not as a general screening question."}
{"qid": 140, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a common ethics question (APA Ethics Code): sliding fee scales based on ability to pay are ethical and are used to make services accessible, provided fees are set at the outset and are not exploitative.\n\nFINAL: Ethical \u2013 sliding fee scales based on ability to pay are acceptable as long as fee arrangements are established early and are not exploitative.", "final": "Ethical \u2013 sliding fee scales based on ability to pay are acceptable as long as fee arrangements are established early and are not exploitative."}
{"qid": 141, "answered": null, "resp": "This appears to be an incomplete question stem about moderate intellectual disability (IQ roughly 35\u201355); in classic psychology/special education texts, individuals with moderate mental retardation are typically described as able to learn basic self-care and communication skills and to perform simple, supervised work (often in sheltered workshops), but generally unable to live fully independently, functioning academically around a second-grade level.\n\nFINAL: Individuals with moderate mental retardation (IQ ~35\u201355) can learn self-care, communication, and simple work skills under supervision (e.g., sheltered workshops), reaching roughly a second-grade academic level, but usually need lifelong support and cannot live fully independently.", "final": "Individuals with moderate mental retardation (IQ ~35\u201355) can learn self-care, communication, and simple work skills under supervision (e.g., sheltered workshops), reaching roughly a second-grade academic level, but usually need lifelong support and cannot live fully independently."}
{"qid": 142, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Parents differentially reinforcing and modeling gender-typed behavior (rough play with boys, protectiveness with girls) reflects the environmental shaping emphasized by social learning theory of gender-role development.\n\nFINAL: Social learning theory", "final": "Social learning theory"}
{"qid": 143, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "This is a classic I/O psychology question: when adverse impact is shown, employers must demonstrate job-relatedness/business necessity, and work sample tests (content-valid, closely mirroring actual job tasks) are most defensible in court compared to aptitude, personality, or general cognitive ability tests.\n\nFINAL: A work sample (job-related, content-valid) test", "final": "A work sample (job-related, content-valid) test"}
{"qid": 144, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "No answer options were provided, but the classic answer is the Kuder Occupational Interest Survey (or Kuder Preference Record), which is based on its own empirical scales rather than Holland's RIASEC typology, unlike the Strong Interest Inventory, Self-Directed Search, and Vocational Preference Inventory.\n\nFINAL: Kuder Occupational Interest Survey", "final": "Kuder Occupational Interest Survey"}
{"qid": 145, "answered": null, "resp": "In House's Path-Goal Theory, instrumental (directive) leadership involves the leader clarifying expectations, giving specific guidance, scheduling work, setting standards, and telling subordinates exactly what is to be done and how \u2014 reducing role ambiguity. It is most effective when tasks are unstructured or ambiguous and subordinates are inexperienced or lack confidence, but can be redundant or demotivating when tasks are already clear and structured.\n\nFINAL: Instrumental (directive) leadership is a task-oriented style in which the leader gives specific guidance, clarifies roles and expectations, plans and schedules work, sets performance standards, and enforces rules \u2014 it maximizes satisfaction/motivation when tasks are ambiguous or unstructured and subordinates need direction, but is less effective (even counterproductive) when tasks are clear and workers are experienced.", "final": "Instrumental (directive) leadership is a task-oriented style in which the leader gives specific guidance, clarifies roles and expectations, plans and schedules work, sets performance standards, and enforces rules \u2014 it maximizes satisfaction/motivation when tasks are ambiguous or unstructured and subordinates need direction, but is less effective (even counterproductive) when tasks are clear and workers are experienced."}
{"qid": 146, "answered": null, "resp": "This appears to be a multiple-choice question stem without listed options; the standard answer is that lifespan developmental theories like Baltes's SOC theory view development as a lifelong, multidimensional, multidirectional process involving both gains and losses, with individuals adapting by selecting goals, optimizing resources, and compensating for declines (i.e., successful aging).\n\nFINAL: They emphasize that development is a lifelong process involving both gains and losses, in which people adapt to age-related declines by selecting goals, optimizing their remaining abilities, and compensating for losses\u2014describing successful aging as adaptive management of change.", "final": "They emphasize that development is a lifelong process involving both gains and losses, in which people adapt to age-related declines by selecting goals, optimizing their remaining abilities, and compensating for losses\u2014describing successful aging as adaptive management of change."}
{"qid": 147, "answered": null, "resp": "Low self-monitors, per Snyder, are guided by their own internal states\u2014attitudes, values, and dispositions\u2014rather than by situational cues or others' expectations, so their behavior tends to be consistent across situations.\n\nFINAL: Their own inner attitudes, values, and feelings (internal dispositions), rather than situational cues or others' expectations.", "final": "Their own inner attitudes, values, and feelings (internal dispositions), rather than situational cues or others' expectations."}
{"qid": 148, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a psychology question about prosocial behavior; research (e.g., bystander effect, Latan\u00e9 and Darley) shows that helping increases when responsibility is clearly assigned and people feel personally accountable, and also when helping norms are modeled. Without the listed options, the best-supported answer is to make people feel personally responsible for helping (e.g., by singling them out or reducing diffusion of responsibility), often paired with teaching/modeling helping.\n\nFINAL: Increase people's sense of personal responsibility for helping (e.g., reduce diffusion of responsibility by assigning responsibility to specific individuals/modeling helping behavior)", "final": "Increase people's sense of personal responsibility for helping (e.g., reduce diffusion of responsibility by assigning responsibility to specific individuals/modeling helping behavior)"}
{"qid": 149, "answered": null, "resp": "Research shows that explicit and implicit memory are dissociable systems: amnesic patients (e.g., with hippocampal damage) show severely impaired explicit memory but preserved implicit memory (priming, procedural learning), and the two develop and age differently. Thus explicit memory depends on hippocampal/medial temporal structures while implicit memory relies on other brain regions and remains relatively intact.\n\nFINAL: Explicit and implicit memory are separate, dissociable systems\u2014explicit memory (dependent on the hippocampus/medial temporal lobe) can be severely impaired, as in amnesia, while implicit memory (priming, skills, conditioning, mediated by other brain regions) remains intact; explicit memory also declines more with aging and develops later in infancy than implicit memory.", "final": "Explicit and implicit memory are separate, dissociable systems\u2014explicit memory (dependent on the hippocampus/medial temporal lobe) can be severely impaired, as in amnesia, while implicit memory (priming, skills, conditioning, mediated by other brain regions) remains intact; explicit memory also declines more with aging and develops later in infancy than implicit memory."}
{"qid": 150, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic psychology/industrial-organizational textbook item; the typical answer is that job satisfaction tends to increase with age (and is only modestly related to performance). Older workers generally report higher job satisfaction than younger workers.\n\nFINAL: increases with age (older workers tend to report greater job satisfaction than younger workers).", "final": "increases with age (older workers tend to report greater job satisfaction than younger workers)."}
{"qid": 151, "answered": null, "resp": "Inoculation theory (McGuire) holds that exposing people to a weakened form of an opposing argument, along with a refutation, builds resistance to later persuasion\u2014much like a vaccine.\n\nFINAL: Expose them to a weak version of the persuasive argument (and refute it) beforehand, so they build up counterarguments and resist the later, stronger message.", "final": "Expose them to a weak version of the persuasive argument (and refute it) beforehand, so they build up counterarguments and resist the later, stronger message."}
{"qid": 152, "answered": null, "resp": "This refers to the Yerkes-Dodson law / inverted-U relationship between arousal and performance, where optimal learning and performance occur at moderate (intermediate) levels of arousal or motivation\u2014too little leads to boredom and too much leads to anxiety that impairs performance.\n\nFINAL: Moderate (intermediate) levels of arousal/motivation, per the inverted-U (Yerkes-Dodson) relationship.", "final": "Moderate (intermediate) levels of arousal/motivation, per the inverted-U (Yerkes-Dodson) relationship."}
{"qid": 153, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic ethics question based on APA Ethics Code Standard 10.08: psychologists do not engage in sexual intimacies with former clients for at least two years after termination, and even after two years only in the most unusual circumstances with the burden on the psychologist to demonstrate no exploitation. Since only six months have passed, the psychologist has acted unethically regardless of the brevity of therapy or the client's initiation.\n\nFINAL: Unethically \u2014 the APA Ethics Code (Standard 10.08) prohibits sexual intimacies with former clients for at least two years after termination (and even then only in the most unusual circumstances), so a sexual relationship six months after termination is an ethical violation.", "final": "Unethically \u2014 the APA Ethics Code (Standard 10.08) prohibits sexual intimacies with former clients for at least two years after termination (and even then only in the most unusual circumstances), so a sexual relationship six months after termination is an ethical violation."}
{"qid": 154, "answered": null, "resp": "No answer options were provided, but common criticisms of Piaget include underestimating children's abilities, overlooking cultural/social influences, small unrepresentative samples, and stages being less discrete than claimed; a statement that is NOT a criticism would be something like \"he overestimated children's abilities\" or \"his theory relied on large, diverse samples.\"\n\nFINAL: The option claiming Piaget overestimated children's cognitive abilities (or used large representative samples) is NOT a criticism \u2014 critics argue he underestimated them.", "final": "The option claiming Piaget overestimated children's cognitive abilities (or used large representative samples) is NOT a criticism \u2014 critics argue he underestimated them."}
{"qid": 155, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Maslow's hierarchy of needs arranges motives in a pyramid where basic physiological and safety needs must be satisfied before higher needs like belonging, esteem, and self-actualization, reflecting that some motives are more essential to survival than others.\n\nFINAL: Maslow's hierarchy of needs (the hierarchical approach to motivation)", "final": "Maslow's hierarchy of needs (the hierarchical approach to motivation)"}
{"qid": 156, "answered": null, "resp": "Senses are commonly classified by the type of stimulus they detect: physical energy (light, sound, pressure, temperature) versus chemical substances (tastes and odors). This yields the two major categories of physical senses (sight, hearing, touch) and chemical senses (taste and smell).\n\nFINAL: Physical senses (sight, hearing, touch) and chemical senses (taste and smell)", "final": "Physical senses (sight, hearing, touch) and chemical senses (taste and smell)"}
{"qid": 157, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "This finding reflects the idea that language shapes thought \u2014 the linguistic relativity (Sapir-Whorf) hypothesis.\n\nFINAL: the linguistic relativity hypothesis (Sapir-Whorf hypothesis) \u2014 the idea that language influences thought", "final": "the linguistic relativity hypothesis (Sapir-Whorf hypothesis) \u2014 the idea that language influences thought"}
{"qid": 158, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic AP Psychology question; the standard options are: teaching relaxation, constructing an anxiety hierarchy, progressively exposing the client to feared stimuli while relaxed, and (the exception) having the client confront the most feared stimulus immediately/directly \u2014 which is flooding, not systematic desensitization.\n\nFINAL: Having the client immediately confront the most anxiety-producing stimulus (flooding) \u2014 this is not a step in systematic desensitization.", "final": "Having the client immediately confront the most anxiety-producing stimulus (flooding) \u2014 this is not a step in systematic desensitization."}
