{"qid": 162, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a common AP Psychology/statistics question: \"whether or not a person eats breakfast\" is a characteristic measured on participants at the outset, not manipulated by the researcher, so it's a variable\u2014specifically a categorical (qualitative/dichotomous) variable, often used as a subject/participant variable or independent variable in a correlational design.\n\nFINAL: categorical (qualitative) variable", "final": "categorical (qualitative) variable"}
{"qid": 163, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Waking after about an hour of napping typically means being roused from deep slow-wave (stage 3/4 NREM) sleep, which causes sleep inertia and grogginess; this stage is characterized by delta waves.\n\nFINAL: delta waves (deep slow-wave NREM sleep)", "final": "delta waves (deep slow-wave NREM sleep)"}
{"qid": 164, "answered": null, "resp": "Deindividuation is a psychological state in which people in groups or under anonymity lose self-awareness, personal identity, and sense of individual responsibility, weakening normal inhibitions and making them more likely to act impulsively or antisocially. It has been tested experimentally by manipulating anonymity and group immersion\u2014e.g., Zimbardo's 1969 study where hooded, anonymous participants delivered longer shocks than identifiable ones; Diener et al.'s 1976 Halloween study where anonymous, group trick-or-treaters stole more candy; and Johnson & Downing's 1979 study showing that anonymity in KKK robes increased aggression while anonymity in nurse uniforms decreased it (suggesting cues, not just anonymity, matter), alongside Zimbardo's Stanford Prison Experiment and field studies of crowd behavior.\n\nFINAL: Deindividuation is the loss of self-awareness, personal identity, and individual accountability that occurs in groups or under anonymity, reducing inhibitions and increasing impulsive/antisocial behavior; it has been tested through experiments manipulating anonymity and group immersion (e.g., Zimbardo's hooded-shock study, Diener's Halloween candy-theft study, Johnson & Downing's KKK-vs-nurse costume study, and the Stanford Prison Experiment), which showed anonymity/group settings increase antisocial behavior, though later work showed situational cues and group norms shape the direction of the effect.", "final": "Deindividuation is the loss of self-awareness, personal identity, and individual accountability that occurs in groups or under anonymity, reducing inhibitions and increasing impulsive/antisocial behavior; it has been tested through experiments manipulating anonymity and group immersion (e.g., Zimbardo's hooded-shock study, Diener's Halloween candy-theft study, Johnson & Downing's KKK-vs-nurse costume study, and the Stanford Prison Experiment), which showed anonymity/group settings increase antisocial behavior, though later work showed situational cues and group norms shape the direction of the effect."}
