{"qid": 192, "answered": null, "resp": "This appears to be a multiple-choice question but the answer options weren't included, so I cannot identify the specific exception; the reticular formation/RAS is associated with arousal, wakefulness/sleep-wake regulation, consciousness, attention, muscle tone/posture, and autonomic functions (cardiovascular, respiratory), and typically the \"except\" answer would be a function like memory storage, higher cognition (e.g., language), or fine motor coordination, which are not RAS functions.\n\nFINAL: Options were not provided; the exception is likely a function such as memory formation/higher cognition (e.g., language or fine motor coordination), since the RAS mediates arousal, wakefulness, attention, muscle tone, and autonomic regulation.", "final": "Options were not provided; the exception is likely a function such as memory formation/higher cognition (e.g., language or fine motor coordination), since the RAS mediates arousal, wakefulness, attention, muscle tone, and autonomic regulation."}
{"qid": 193, "answered": null, "resp": "The question references answer options (\"the following ones\") that were not included, so I can't identify the incorrectly defined factor directly; however, the most commonly mis-stated factor in such questions is the claim that power decreases as sample size increases (in fact, larger sample size increases power), or that power decreases when alpha increases (in fact, a larger alpha increases power). Correct definitions: power increases with larger sample size, larger effect size, larger alpha, smaller variability, and a one-tailed test in the correct direction.\n\nFINAL: The options were not provided; the likely incorrectly defined factor is the one stating that power decreases with a larger sample size (or with a larger alpha) \u2014 in reality, larger sample size, larger alpha, larger effect size, and smaller variability all increase power.", "final": "The options were not provided; the likely incorrectly defined factor is the one stating that power decreases with a larger sample size (or with a larger alpha) \u2014 in reality, larger sample size, larger alpha, larger effect size, and smaller variability all increase power."}
{"qid": 194, "answered": null, "resp": "No options were provided, but based on Schmidt & Hunter's meta-analyses, general mental ability (cognitive ability) tests and work sample tests show the highest predictive validity for job performance, with GMA tests typically cited as the single best predictor across jobs (especially combined with structured interviews or integrity tests).\n\nFINAL: General mental ability (cognitive ability/intelligence) tests", "final": "General mental ability (cognitive ability/intelligence) tests"}
{"qid": 195, "answered": null, "resp": "The denominator of the F-ratio (mean square within/error) is reduced by using homogeneous samples, more reliable measurement, and controlling extraneous variables\u2014i.e., anything that reduces within-group variability (error variance). Larger sample sizes also increase the degrees of freedom, helping estimate error more precisely.\n\nFINAL: Reducing within-group (error) variability\u2014e.g., by using homogeneous subjects, controlling extraneous variables, and using reliable measurement/larger samples.", "final": "Reducing within-group (error) variability\u2014e.g., by using homogeneous subjects, controlling extraneous variables, and using reliable measurement/larger samples."}
{"qid": 196, "answered": null, "resp": "The first standardized measure of assessment is generally credited to Alfred Binet and Th\u00e9odore Simon, who developed the Binet-Simon Intelligence Scale in 1905 to identify French schoolchildren needing special educational help.\n\nFINAL: The Binet-Simon Intelligence Scale (1905), developed by Alfred Binet and Th\u00e9odore Simon", "final": "The Binet-Simon Intelligence Scale (1905), developed by Alfred Binet and Th\u00e9odore Simon"}
{"qid": 197, "answered": null, "resp": "Berkowitz's revision held that frustration produces only a readiness (anger) for aggression, and that actual aggression occurs when aggressive cues in the environment (stimuli associated with aggression or with the frustrater) are present to trigger it.\n\nFINAL: When aggressive cues are present in the environment\u2014frustration creates only a readiness (anger) to aggress, and situational cues associated with aggression (e.g., weapons, the frustrating person) are needed to elicit actual aggressive behavior.", "final": "When aggressive cues are present in the environment\u2014frustration creates only a readiness (anger) to aggress, and situational cues associated with aggression (e.g., weapons, the frustrating person) are needed to elicit actual aggressive behavior."}
