{"qid": 242, "answered": null, "resp": "Clingy anxiety followed by pushing the mother away and avoiding contact, combined with the mother's own turning away, reflects contradictory approach/avoidance behaviors characteristic of disorganized attachment. However, this mix of clinging and rejecting contact is also typical of insecure-resistant (ambivalent) attachment, which is the classic description of alternating seeking and resisting contact\u2014but the mutual disengagement and inconsistent, contradictory behavior point most strongly to disorganized/disoriented attachment.\n\nFINAL: Disorganized (disoriented) attachment", "final": "Disorganized (disoriented) attachment"}
{"qid": 243, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic ethics question about handling a colleague's serious ethical violation (sex with a current client). The APA Ethics Code (Standard 1.04/1.05) directs that when an informal resolution is inappropriate because the violation is serious and has caused substantial harm, the psychologist should report the colleague to the appropriate ethics committee or licensing board\u2014especially since a sexual relationship with a current client is a serious violation not suitable for informal resolution.\n\nFINAL: Report your colleague to the appropriate ethics committee or state licensing board, since a sexual relationship with a current client is a serious violation that is not appropriate for informal resolution (and you should not contact the client yourself, as that would breach confidentiality).", "final": "Report your colleague to the appropriate ethics committee or state licensing board, since a sexual relationship with a current client is a serious violation that is not appropriate for informal resolution (and you should not contact the client yourself, as that would breach confidentiality)."}
{"qid": 244, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a standard EPPP ethics question about EAP confidentiality: information about an employee's participation in EAP counseling can be shared with the supervisor only with the employee's consent (typically a signed release), unless a legally mandated exception applies. The correct choice is that the psychologist can disclose only when the employee has signed a release authorizing it.\n\nFINAL: Only if the employee gives (signed) consent/authorization to release that information.", "final": "Only if the employee gives (signed) consent/authorization to release that information."}
{"qid": 245, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "In classical psychoanalytic theory, the symptom is understood as a compromise formation between the repressed unconscious impulse (id wish) and the defense mounted against it, allowing partial, disguised expression of the impulse.\n\nFINAL: a symptom (a \"compromise formation\" \u2014 the neurotic symptom)", "final": "a symptom (a \"compromise formation\" \u2014 the neurotic symptom)"}
{"qid": 246, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "The in-basket exercise simulates managerial tasks (memos, reports, phone messages requiring prioritization and decisions), so it is used mainly to assess candidates for managerial/executive positions.\n\nFINAL: Managerial/executive (administrative) employees", "final": "Managerial/executive (administrative) employees"}
{"qid": 247, "answered": null, "resp": "The question lists no options, so I'll give the classic example: a double-bind message is one where verbal and nonverbal (or two simultaneous) messages contradict each other and the receiver cannot escape or comment on the contradiction\u2014e.g., a parent says \"Come give me a hug, I love you\" while stiffening and pulling away when the child approaches, or a spouse says \"Of course I'm not angry\" through clenched teeth and slammed doors.\n\nFINAL: A message whose verbal content contradicts its nonverbal/contextual content, leaving the receiver unable to respond correctly\u2014e.g., a mother saying \"I love you, come here\" while physically recoiling from her child.", "final": "A message whose verbal content contradicts its nonverbal/contextual content, leaving the receiver unable to respond correctly\u2014e.g., a mother saying \"I love you, come here\" while physically recoiling from her child."}
{"qid": 248, "answered": null, "resp": "The projective hypothesis (Frank, 1939) holds that when people respond to ambiguous stimuli, they \"project\" their own unconscious needs, feelings, and conflicts onto the material, so their responses reveal aspects of personality they may not be aware of or willing to disclose. The statement most closely related would be one asserting that responses to ambiguous stimuli reflect unconscious aspects of the individual's personality.\n\nFINAL: The statement that people's responses to ambiguous stimuli reveal their unconscious needs, motives, and conflicts (i.e., they project their inner personality onto the ambiguous material).", "final": "The statement that people's responses to ambiguous stimuli reveal their unconscious needs, motives, and conflicts (i.e., they project their inner personality onto the ambiguous material)."}
{"qid": 249, "answered": null, "resp": "This looks like a multiple-choice question without options provided; the standard answer is that extinction is not the treatment of choice when the behavior is dangerous/harmful (e.g., self-injurious or aggressive behavior), because extinction produces an initial extinction burst and the behavior cannot be safely tolerated while it runs its course, or when the reinforcer cannot be withheld.\n\nFINAL: When the target behavior is dangerous (e.g., self-injurious or aggressive), since extinction bursts could cause serious harm and the behavior can't be safely ignored.", "final": "When the target behavior is dangerous (e.g., self-injurious or aggressive), since extinction bursts could cause serious harm and the behavior can't be safely ignored."}
{"qid": 250, "answered": null, "resp": "The DSM-5 criteria for gambling disorder include tolerance, withdrawal (restlessness/irritability), loss of control, preoccupation, escape, chasing losses, lying, jeopardizing relationships/jobs, and bailouts\u2014but \"committing illegal acts to finance gambling,\" which was in DSM-IV, was removed in DSM-5. Since no options were provided, the most commonly cited omitted item is illegal acts.\n\nFINAL: Committing illegal acts (e.g., forgery, fraud, theft) to finance gambling \u2014 this DSM-IV criterion was removed from DSM-5.", "final": "Committing illegal acts (e.g., forgery, fraud, theft) to finance gambling \u2014 this DSM-IV criterion was removed from DSM-5."}
{"qid": 251, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic psychology exam question referencing the meta-analysis (e.g., Reid & Crisafulli, 1990) on marital discord and child behavior problems, which found a relatively weak overall association, with the effect somewhat stronger for boys (particularly conduct problems) than for girls; the expected answer is that the boy would be more likely than the girl to show behavior problems.\n\nFINAL: The boy would be more likely than the girl to develop behavior problems (especially externalizing/conduct problems), though the overall relationship between marital discord and child behavior problems is modest.", "final": "The boy would be more likely than the girl to develop behavior problems (especially externalizing/conduct problems), though the overall relationship between marital discord and child behavior problems is modest."}
{"qid": 252, "answered": null, "resp": "Howard et al.'s (1996) phase model proposes that psychotherapy progresses through three sequential phases: remoralization (improved subjective well-being, hopefulness), then remediation (symptom reduction), then rehabilitation (improved life functioning). In the first few sessions, remoralization occurs, so subjective well-being/hopefulness is most likely to be affected first.\n\nFINAL: Subjective well-being (remoralization\u2014increased hope/morale), which improves before symptom reduction and life functioning.", "final": "Subjective well-being (remoralization\u2014increased hope/morale), which improves before symptom reduction and life functioning."}
{"qid": 253, "answered": null, "resp": "Heteroscedasticity means the spread (variance) of Y scores differs across levels of X, so the dots form an unequal band (e.g., fan or funnel shape) rather than a uniform band around the regression line, indicating that prediction accuracy (standard error of estimate) is not constant across all values of X.\n\nFINAL: The variability of Y scores is not the same at all values of X \u2014 the scatter of points around the regression line is unequal (e.g., a fan/funnel shape), so the standard error of estimate/prediction accuracy differs across the range of X rather than being homogeneous.", "final": "The variability of Y scores is not the same at all values of X \u2014 the scatter of points around the regression line is unequal (e.g., a fan/funnel shape), so the standard error of estimate/prediction accuracy differs across the range of X rather than being homogeneous."}
