{"qid": 256, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Selye's GAS has three stages: alarm, resistance, and exhaustion. During the exhaustion stage, the body's resources are depleted and immune functioning is compromised, making us most susceptible to disease.\n\nFINAL: the exhaustion stage", "final": "the exhaustion stage"}
{"qid": 257, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Soledad responds (reinforces) every single e-mail her friend sends, so each behavior is reinforced without exception, which is continuous reinforcement (a fixed-ratio 1 schedule).\n\nFINAL: Continuous reinforcement (fixed-ratio 1 schedule)", "final": "Continuous reinforcement (fixed-ratio 1 schedule)"}
{"qid": 258, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Proactive interference occurs when previously learned material disrupts the recall of newer material, so only lists learned before list 2 can proactively interfere with it. Lists 3 and 4 would instead cause retroactive interference.\n\nFINAL: List 1", "final": "List 1"}
{"qid": 259, "answered": null, "resp": "The Whorfian hypothesis (linguistic relativity) holds that the structure and vocabulary of one's language shape how one perceives and thinks about the world, so speakers of different languages may categorize experience differently. This is why, for example, people who speak languages with more color terms can more readily distinguish and remember those color categories, and why translation across languages is often imperfect.\n\nFINAL: Speakers of different languages perceive, categorize, and think about the world differently\u2014because the language one speaks shapes (and constrains) one's thought and perception, making exact translation across languages difficult.", "final": "Speakers of different languages perceive, categorize, and think about the world differently\u2014because the language one speaks shapes (and constrains) one's thought and perception, making exact translation across languages difficult."}
{"qid": 260, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Scratching activates large-diameter touch fibers that inhibit transmission of pain signals in the spinal cord, closing the \"gate\" so pain is not perceived until the stimulation stops. This is the gate-control theory of pain (Melzack and Wall).\n\nFINAL: Gate-control theory of pain", "final": "Gate-control theory of pain"}
{"qid": 261, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Functional imaging like PET or fMRI shows metabolic/blood-flow activity across the entire brain during tasks, demonstrating that virtually all regions are active over time, unlike structural scans (CT, MRI) that only show anatomy.\n\nFINAL: A functional scan such as PET (or fMRI), which shows activity throughout the whole brain rather than just structure.", "final": "A functional scan such as PET (or fMRI), which shows activity throughout the whole brain rather than just structure."}
{"qid": 262, "answered": null, "resp": "Developmental psychologists most commonly rely on observational and descriptive methods (naturalistic observation, interviews, and longitudinal/cross-sectional studies) rather than experiments, since many developmental variables cannot be manipulated ethically; among these, observation is typically cited as the primary method.\n\nFINAL: Observation (naturalistic/systematic observation, often via longitudinal and cross-sectional studies)", "final": "Observation (naturalistic/systematic observation, often via longitudinal and cross-sectional studies)"}
{"qid": 263, "answered": null, "resp": "The reflex arc begins with a stimulus detected by a receptor, travels along a sensory (afferent) neuron to the CNS, crosses to a motor (efferent) neuron (often via an interneuron), and ends at an effector (muscle or gland) that produces the response.\n\nFINAL: Receptor \u2192 sensory (afferent) neuron \u2192 CNS (spinal cord/interneuron) \u2192 motor (efferent) neuron \u2192 effector (muscle or gland)", "final": "Receptor \u2192 sensory (afferent) neuron \u2192 CNS (spinal cord/interneuron) \u2192 motor (efferent) neuron \u2192 effector (muscle or gland)"}
{"qid": 264, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Jeopardy! tests recall of accumulated facts, vocabulary, and general knowledge, which is the hallmark of crystallized intelligence (as opposed to fluid intelligence, which involves novel problem-solving).\n\nFINAL: Crystallized intelligence", "final": "Crystallized intelligence"}
{"qid": 265, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Hypnotizability is most consistently linked to absorption and imaginative involvement, which fall under the Big Five dimension of Openness to Experience.\n\nFINAL: Openness to experience", "final": "Openness to experience"}
{"qid": 266, "answered": null, "resp": "\"Areinforcer\" appears to be a typo for \"a reinforcer,\" a term from behavioral psychology (operant conditioning). A reinforcer is any stimulus or event that, when it follows a behavior, increases the likelihood that the behavior will occur again\u2014e.g., food, praise, or the removal of something unpleasant.\n\nFINAL: A reinforcer is a stimulus or consequence that follows a behavior and increases the probability that the behavior will be repeated (positive reinforcers add something desirable; negative reinforcers remove something aversive).", "final": "A reinforcer is a stimulus or consequence that follows a behavior and increases the probability that the behavior will be repeated (positive reinforcers add something desirable; negative reinforcers remove something aversive)."}
{"qid": 267, "answered": null, "resp": "Freud's psychoanalytic theory has been criticized for being unscientific and unfalsifiable (Popper), since concepts like the unconscious, repression, and the Oedipus complex cannot be empirically tested and any outcome can be explained after the fact. Critics also note his overemphasis on sexuality and childhood, his reliance on a small, biased sample of case studies (mostly middle-class Viennese women) with possible suggestive interpretation, his male-centered and sexist views (e.g., \"penis envy\"), cultural specificity that ignores social factors (Horney, Fromm), and the fact that psychoanalytic therapy is lengthy and shows little clear evidence of superior effectiveness.\n\nFINAL: Freud's theory is criticized as unscientific and unfalsifiable, overemphasizing sex and childhood, based on unrepresentative and possibly suggestive case studies, sexist and culturally biased, and lacking evidence of therapeutic effectiveness.", "final": "Freud's theory is criticized as unscientific and unfalsifiable, overemphasizing sex and childhood, based on unrepresentative and possibly suggestive case studies, sexist and culturally biased, and lacking evidence of therapeutic effectiveness."}
{"qid": 268, "answered": null, "resp": "Money is not valued for its own sake but as a means to obtain other things, so it is an instrumental (extrinsic) goal rather than an intrinsic or terminal one.\n\nFINAL: An instrumental (extrinsic) goal \u2014 a means to other ends rather than an end in itself.", "final": "An instrumental (extrinsic) goal \u2014 a means to other ends rather than an end in itself."}
{"qid": 269, "answered": null, "resp": "Attitudes are learned through classical conditioning (associating an attitude object with positive or negative stimuli/emotions), operant/instrumental conditioning (attitudes reinforced by rewards or punishment, e.g., praise or approval), and observational learning/modeling (acquiring attitudes by watching and imitating parents, peers, and media figures).\n\nFINAL: Classical conditioning (association of an object with pleasant/unpleasant stimuli), operant (instrumental) conditioning (reinforcement or punishment of expressed attitudes), and observational learning/modeling (imitating attitudes of parents, peers, and media).", "final": "Classical conditioning (association of an object with pleasant/unpleasant stimuli), operant (instrumental) conditioning (reinforcement or punishment of expressed attitudes), and observational learning/modeling (imitating attitudes of parents, peers, and media)."}
{"qid": 270, "answered": null, "resp": "Fatigue\u2014physical or mental exhaustion from inadequate sleep, long shifts, shift work, monotonous tasks, or heavy exertion\u2014degrades alertness, reaction time, judgment, memory, and coordination, with effects comparable to alcohol impairment (e.g., 17\u201319 hours awake \u2248 0.05% BAC). Because of this, fatigued workers are far more likely to make errors, miss hazards, take shortcuts, or fall asleep, making fatigue a major contributing factor in workplace accidents (notably in transportation, healthcare, and industry\u2014e.g., Chernobyl, Exxon Valdez, Three Mile Island), which employers address through hours-of-service limits, scheduling, rest breaks, and fatigue-risk management systems.\n\nFINAL: Fatigue is a state of reduced physical and mental capacity caused by lack of sleep, long or irregular hours, shift work, and demanding tasks; it slows reaction time, impairs judgment, attention, and coordination (similar to alcohol impairment), and thus significantly increases the likelihood of errors and workplace accidents\u2014being implicated in many industrial and transportation disasters\u2014so it must be managed through work-hour limits, rest breaks, proper scheduling, and fatigue-risk management programs.", "final": "Fatigue is a state of reduced physical and mental capacity caused by lack of sleep, long or irregular hours, shift work, and demanding tasks; it slows reaction time, impairs judgment, attention, and coordination (similar to alcohol impairment), and thus significantly increases the likelihood of errors and workplace accidents\u2014being implicated in many industrial and transportation disasters\u2014so it must be managed through work-hour limits, rest breaks, proper scheduling, and fatigue-risk management programs."}
