{"qid": 273, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a standard psychology/education question on concept attainment: concepts can be acquired through (1) direct sensory experience and observation of examples (discovery/inductive learning), (2) verbal definition or explanation from teachers/texts (deductive/expository learning), and (3) comparing positive and negative instances to abstract common attributes (Bruner's concept attainment strategy via hypothesis testing and feedback).\n\nFINAL: Concepts can be attained in three main ways: (1) Through direct experience/discovery \u2014 the learner observes and interacts with many concrete examples (e.g., seeing many dogs) and inductively abstracts their shared attributes to form the concept; (2) Through definition/verbal instruction \u2014 the concept is presented deductively by a teacher or text as a rule or definition listing its critical attributes, which the learner then applies to identify new instances; and (3) Through comparison of positive and negative examples (Bruner's concept attainment) \u2014 the learner contrasts instances that belong to the concept with those that do not, forms hypotheses about the defining features, and tests/refines them with feedback until the concept's essential attributes are identified.", "final": "Concepts can be attained in three main ways: (1) Through direct experience/discovery \u2014 the learner observes and interacts with many concrete examples (e.g., seeing many dogs) and inductively abstracts their shared attributes to form the concept; (2) Through definition/verbal instruction \u2014 the concept is presented deductively by a teacher or text as a rule or definition listing its critical attributes, which the learner then applies to identify new instances; and (3) Through comparison of positive and negative examples (Bruner's concept attainment) \u2014 the learner contrasts instances that belong to the concept with those that do not, forms hypotheses about the defining features, and tests/refines them with feedback until the concept's essential attributes are identified."}
{"qid": 274, "answered": null, "resp": "\"Autonomic conditioning\" refers to the learned modification of autonomic (involuntary) physiological responses\u2014such as heart rate, blood pressure, skin conductance, salivation, or pupil dilation\u2014through classical (Pavlovian) or operant/instrumental conditioning, the latter often via biofeedback (notably studied by Neal Miller). In classical autonomic conditioning, a neutral stimulus paired with one that naturally elicits an autonomic reaction comes to trigger that reaction itself (e.g., Pavlov's dogs salivating to a bell).\n\nFINAL: Autonomic conditioning is the learned alteration of involuntary autonomic nervous system responses (e.g., heart rate, blood pressure, salivation, sweating) through classical or operant conditioning\u2014as when a neutral stimulus comes to elicit a visceral response after pairing with a natural trigger, or when biofeedback trains voluntary-like control of such responses.", "final": "Autonomic conditioning is the learned alteration of involuntary autonomic nervous system responses (e.g., heart rate, blood pressure, salivation, sweating) through classical or operant conditioning\u2014as when a neutral stimulus comes to elicit a visceral response after pairing with a natural trigger, or when biofeedback trains voluntary-like control of such responses."}
{"qid": 275, "answered": null, "resp": "Piaget's fourth stage, formal operations, begins around age 11-12 and characterizes adolescence: the adolescent gains the capacity for abstract, hypothetico-deductive reasoning, can think about possibilities rather than only concrete realities, systematically tests hypotheses (as in the pendulum problem), uses propositional logic, reflects on their own thinking (metacognition), and can consider ideals, ethics, and the future. This new abstract capacity also produces a renewed adolescent egocentrism\u2014idealism, argumentativeness, and a self-focused sense that others share their preoccupations\u2014which gradually diminishes as formal thought matures.\n\nFINAL: Piaget described adolescence as the stage of formal operational thought (beginning ~11-12), marked by abstract, hypothetical-deductive and propositional reasoning, systematic hypothesis testing, metacognition, and consideration of possibilities and ideals rather than concrete reality alone; this new power is initially accompanied by adolescent egocentrism and idealism that fade as formal thinking matures.", "final": "Piaget described adolescence as the stage of formal operational thought (beginning ~11-12), marked by abstract, hypothetical-deductive and propositional reasoning, systematic hypothesis testing, metacognition, and consideration of possibilities and ideals rather than concrete reality alone; this new power is initially accompanied by adolescent egocentrism and idealism that fade as formal thinking matures."}
{"qid": 276, "answered": null, "resp": "Self-theory refers to the set of beliefs a person holds about themselves\u2014their abilities, traits, and nature\u2014as developed in psychology, notably Carol Dweck's work distinguishing \"entity\" (fixed) versus \"incremental\" (growth) self-theories about whether personal attributes are fixed or malleable. More broadly, in personality/humanistic psychology (e.g., Carl Rogers), self-theory describes how the self-concept forms, organizes experience, and guides behavior and motivation.\n\nFINAL: Self-theory is the psychological concept of the implicit beliefs (a \"theory\") people hold about themselves and their attributes\u2014especially, per Carol Dweck, whether abilities are fixed (entity theory) or changeable (incremental/growth theory)\u2014which shape motivation, goals, and behavior; more broadly (e.g., Carl Rogers), it denotes theories of how the self-concept develops and organizes experience.", "final": "Self-theory is the psychological concept of the implicit beliefs (a \"theory\") people hold about themselves and their attributes\u2014especially, per Carol Dweck, whether abilities are fixed (entity theory) or changeable (incremental/growth theory)\u2014which shape motivation, goals, and behavior; more broadly (e.g., Carl Rogers), it denotes theories of how the self-concept develops and organizes experience."}
{"qid": 277, "answered": null, "resp": "Labor complications can affect the mother, the fetus, or both, and arise from problems with the \"powers\" (uterine contractions), the \"passenger\" (fetus), or the \"passage\" (maternal pelvis/birth canal), as well as from bleeding, infection, and maternal medical conditions. Key examples include prolonged or arrested labor (dystocia) from inadequate contractions or cephalopelvic disproportion, malpresentation (breech, transverse lie), fetal distress/hypoxia with abnormal heart rate patterns, umbilical cord prolapse, shoulder dystocia, uterine rupture, placental abruption or placenta previa causing hemorrhage, premature rupture of membranes and chorioamnionitis, preterm labor, meconium aspiration, preeclampsia/eclampsia, amniotic fluid embolism, retained placenta, and postpartum hemorrhage from uterine atony or perineal/cervical lacerations.\n\nFINAL: Complications during labor include dystocia (prolonged/obstructed labor from weak contractions or cephalopelvic disproportion), fetal malpresentation (breech/transverse), fetal distress and hypoxia, umbilical cord prolapse or compression, shoulder dystocia, uterine rupture, placental abruption or previa with hemorrhage, premature rupture of membranes and chorioamnionitis (infection), preterm labor, meconium aspiration, preeclampsia/eclampsia, amniotic fluid embolism, retained placenta, perineal/cervical lacerations, and postpartum hemorrhage\u2014many requiring interventions such as augmentation, instrumental delivery, or emergency cesarean section.", "final": "Complications during labor include dystocia (prolonged/obstructed labor from weak contractions or cephalopelvic disproportion), fetal malpresentation (breech/transverse), fetal distress and hypoxia, umbilical cord prolapse or compression, shoulder dystocia, uterine rupture, placental abruption or previa with hemorrhage, premature rupture of membranes and chorioamnionitis (infection), preterm labor, meconium aspiration, preeclampsia/eclampsia, amniotic fluid embolism, retained placenta, perineal/cervical lacerations, and postpartum hemorrhage\u2014many requiring interventions such as augmentation, instrumental delivery, or emergency cesarean section."}
{"qid": 278, "answered": null, "resp": "Emotion is a psychological state that combines a subjective feeling (how the experience is consciously felt, e.g., joy or fear), a response (physiological arousal such as heart rate changes and expressive/behavioral reactions like facial expressions), and motivation (an impulse or drive that pushes us toward or away from certain actions, such as fleeing danger or approaching a reward). In short, emotion is felt, expressed bodily and behaviorally, and energizes and directs behavior.\n\nFINAL: Emotion is a complex psychological state consisting of (1) a subjective feeling component\u2014the conscious experience of the emotion (e.g., feeling happy, afraid, angry); (2) a response component\u2014physiological arousal (e.g., increased heart rate, hormone release) and expressive/behavioral reactions (e.g., facial expressions, body language); and (3) a motivational component\u2014an impulse or drive that energizes and directs behavior toward or away from something (e.g., fear motivates escape, love motivates approach). Emotions thus involve how we feel, how our body and behavior react, and what we are moved to do.", "final": "Emotion is a complex psychological state consisting of (1) a subjective feeling component\u2014the conscious experience of the emotion (e.g., feeling happy, afraid, angry); (2) a response component\u2014physiological arousal (e.g., increased heart rate, hormone release) and expressive/behavioral reactions (e.g., facial expressions, body language); and (3) a motivational component\u2014an impulse or drive that energizes and directs behavior toward or away from something (e.g., fear motivates escape, love motivates approach). Emotions thus involve how we feel, how our body and behavior react, and what we are moved to do."}
{"qid": 279, "answered": null, "resp": "Selecting a manager requires a multi-stage process because the role demands leadership, judgment, and interpersonal skills that a single test cannot reveal: begin with job analysis to define required competencies, then screen applications/r\u00e9sum\u00e9s, use preliminary and structured behavioral interviews, administer aptitude/personality and situational tests (e.g., in-basket exercises, case studies, assessment centers, leadership role-plays), conduct panel/final interviews with senior management, verify references and background, complete a medical check, and finally make the offer with a probationary period. This layered approach improves validity by triangulating evidence of technical knowledge, decision-making, communication, and cultural fit, reducing costly hiring errors for a high-impact position.\n\nFINAL: Use a sequential, multi-method procedure: job analysis \u2192 application/r\u00e9sum\u00e9 screening \u2192 preliminary interview \u2192 selection tests (aptitude, personality, situational/in-basket exercises, assessment centre) \u2192 structured behavioral and panel interviews \u2192 reference and background checks \u2192 medical examination \u2192 final approval and job offer with probation; this triangulates evidence on leadership, decision-making, and fit, giving a more valid and reliable managerial selection than any single method.", "final": "Use a sequential, multi-method procedure: job analysis \u2192 application/r\u00e9sum\u00e9 screening \u2192 preliminary interview \u2192 selection tests (aptitude, personality, situational/in-basket exercises, assessment centre) \u2192 structured behavioral and panel interviews \u2192 reference and background checks \u2192 medical examination \u2192 final approval and job offer with probation; this triangulates evidence on leadership, decision-making, and fit, giving a more valid and reliable managerial selection than any single method."}
{"qid": 280, "answered": null, "resp": "Criterion-related validity is the extent to which a measure or test correlates with an external criterion\u2014an independent, established measure of the same construct or outcome\u2014thereby showing that scores on the test predict or agree with that criterion. It is typically assessed as concurrent validity (criterion measured at the same time) or predictive validity (criterion measured later).\n\nFINAL: Criterion-related validity is the degree to which a test's scores relate to (correlate with or predict) an external criterion measure of the construct or outcome, assessed either concurrently or predictively.", "final": "Criterion-related validity is the degree to which a test's scores relate to (correlate with or predict) an external criterion measure of the construct or outcome, assessed either concurrently or predictively."}
{"qid": 281, "answered": null, "resp": "Most psychological research on aggression (e.g., Bandura's social learning studies, longitudinal correlational work) finds that viewing violent content increases aggressive thoughts, feelings, and behavior, especially in children. This implies that violent television programming likely contributes to increased aggression in viewers, suggesting a need for caution or reduction in TV violence.\n\nFINAL: They imply that violent television programming increases aggressive behavior in viewers (particularly children) through observational learning/modeling, so exposure to TV violence tends to promote, rather than reduce, aggression.", "final": "They imply that violent television programming increases aggressive behavior in viewers (particularly children) through observational learning/modeling, so exposure to TV violence tends to promote, rather than reduce, aggression."}
{"qid": 282, "answered": null, "resp": "Rosenhan's 1973 study \"On Being Sane in Insane Places\" had healthy confederates feign a single symptom (hearing voices saying \"empty,\" \"hollow,\" \"thud\") to gain admission; once admitted, they behaved normally, yet staff never detected their sanity, interpreted their normal behavior as pathological, and discharged them with diagnoses like \"schizophrenia in remission\" \u2014 though fellow patients often suspected they were not really ill.\n\nFINAL: It showed that hospital staff could not distinguish the sane pseudopatients from genuinely ill patients\u2014their normal behavior was interpreted as symptomatic and they were discharged labeled \"schizophrenia in remission\"\u2014demonstrating the power of diagnostic labels and the unreliability of psychiatric diagnosis (while other patients often detected the deception).", "final": "It showed that hospital staff could not distinguish the sane pseudopatients from genuinely ill patients\u2014their normal behavior was interpreted as symptomatic and they were discharged labeled \"schizophrenia in remission\"\u2014demonstrating the power of diagnostic labels and the unreliability of psychiatric diagnosis (while other patients often detected the deception)."}
{"qid": 283, "answered": null, "resp": "Self-instructional training (Meichenbaum & Goodman) teaches impulsive children to talk to themselves\u2014first aloud, then covertly\u2014to guide, monitor, and control their behavior on tasks.\n\nFINAL: Use self-talk (verbal self-instructions, first overt then covert) to guide and control their own behavior, slowing down and thinking before acting.", "final": "Use self-talk (verbal self-instructions, first overt then covert) to guide and control their own behavior, slowing down and thinking before acting."}
{"qid": 284, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Old learning interfering with the acquisition of new responses is termed proactive interference (proactive inhibition), as opposed to retroactive interference where new learning disrupts recall of old material.\n\nFINAL: Proactive interference (proactive inhibition)", "final": "Proactive interference (proactive inhibition)"}
{"qid": 285, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic ethics exam question: when privilege exists but the specific situation isn't clearly covered, the psychologist should assert privilege on the client's behalf and refuse to testify until the client waives privilege or a court orders testimony, since privilege belongs to the client. Thus the most appropriate action is to claim privilege and decline to testify unless the client consents or the court rules otherwise.\n\nFINAL: Assert (claim) privilege on the client's behalf and decline to testify unless the client waives privilege or a court orders you to testify.", "final": "Assert (claim) privilege on the client's behalf and decline to testify unless the client waives privilege or a court orders you to testify."}
{"qid": 286, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Cattell's theory distinguishes fluid intelligence (nonverbal, relatively culture-free reasoning ability) from crystallized intelligence (acquired knowledge and skills shaped by culture and education).\n\nFINAL: Fluid intelligence (fluid abilities)", "final": "Fluid intelligence (fluid abilities)"}
{"qid": 287, "answered": null, "resp": "Quality assurance focuses on the processes used to produce a product or service, aiming to prevent defects and ensure that quality standards are met before problems arise, rather than merely detecting them afterward (which is quality control).\n\nFINAL: To prevent defects and ensure that products/services consistently meet defined quality standards and customer requirements by focusing on and improving the processes used to create them.", "final": "To prevent defects and ensure that products/services consistently meet defined quality standards and customer requirements by focusing on and improving the processes used to create them."}
{"qid": 288, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "This is a standard multiple-choice ethics question; paying for a legitimate consultation (professional service actually rendered) is ethical, unlike paying for a referral itself (fee-splitting/kickback). Since the therapist is paying for the referring psychologist's time and expertise in consultation, not for the referral, it is acceptable.\n\nFINAL: Ethical, because the payment is for a professional service (the consultation) actually rendered, not a fee for the referral itself.", "final": "Ethical, because the payment is for a professional service (the consultation) actually rendered, not a fee for the referral itself."}
{"qid": 289, "answered": "d2", "resp": "The question references answer options that weren't provided, but standard needs analysis (per training literature) comprises organizational analysis, task/job analysis, and person/individual analysis\u2014so any listed component outside these three (e.g., \"cost-benefit analysis,\" \"performance appraisal,\" or \"training evaluation\") would be the one not part of needs analysis.\n\nFINAL: The component that is not part of Needs Analysis is the one that isn't organizational, task, or person analysis (typically \"training evaluation\"/\"cost-benefit analysis\" in such option lists).", "final": "The component that is not part of Needs Analysis is the one that isn't organizational, task, or person analysis (typically \"training evaluation\"/\"cost-benefit analysis\" in such option lists)."}
{"qid": 290, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic ethics question: with a valid signed release, the psychologist should provide the report, but since it is 7 years old, the psychologist should note that the findings may be outdated and should be interpreted with caution (i.e., send the report with a cover letter/note about its age and possible obsolescence).\n\nFINAL: Send the requested report to the psychiatrist, but include a statement noting that the report is 7 years old and its findings may be outdated and should be interpreted cautiously.", "final": "Send the requested report to the psychiatrist, but include a statement noting that the report is 7 years old and its findings may be outdated and should be interpreted cautiously."}
{"qid": 291, "answered": null, "resp": "The elaboration likelihood model (Petty & Cacioppo) holds that central route processing\u2014careful scrutiny of argument quality\u2014occurs when the person has both the motivation (e.g., high personal relevance/involvement) and the ability (e.g., knowledge, time, no distraction) to think carefully about the message.\n\nFINAL: When the person is both motivated (e.g., the issue is personally relevant/important) and able (has the knowledge, time, and attention) to think carefully about the message's arguments\u2014i.e., when elaboration likelihood is high.", "final": "When the person is both motivated (e.g., the issue is personally relevant/important) and able (has the knowledge, time, and attention) to think carefully about the message's arguments\u2014i.e., when elaboration likelihood is high."}
{"qid": 292, "answered": null, "resp": "The question appears to be truncated\u2014the answer options aren't included\u2014but the standard completion of this analogy pairs sense modalities with their receptor organs: vision is to hearing as eye is to ear (or, in receptor terms, as retina/photoreceptor is to cochlea/hair cell).\n\nFINAL: eye is to ear (vision:hearing :: eye:ear; equivalently retina:cochlea or rod/cone:hair cell)", "final": "eye is to ear (vision:hearing :: eye:ear; equivalently retina:cochlea or rod/cone:hair cell)"}
{"qid": 293, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic textbook question (e.g., from social psychology/gender studies); research (Tannen, Lakoff, etc.) indicates women tend to be more polite, ask more questions, use more tag questions and hedges, are more supportive/relational, and interrupt less, whereas men interrupt more and speak more in mixed-sex groups.\n\nFINAL: Women tend to use more tentative, polite, and affiliative speech\u2014asking more questions, using more hedges and tag questions, giving more supportive listening responses\u2014and interrupt less, while men talk more and interrupt more in mixed-sex conversations.", "final": "Women tend to use more tentative, polite, and affiliative speech\u2014asking more questions, using more hedges and tag questions, giving more supportive listening responses\u2014and interrupt less, while men talk more and interrupt more in mixed-sex conversations."}
{"qid": 294, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic psychometrics question: shrinkage upon cross-validation indicates the original regression weights capitalized on chance (sampling error) \u2014 typically because the original sample was small relative to the number of predictors, so the multiple R was inflated by fitting error-specific idiosyncrasies of that sample.\n\nFINAL: The original multiple R was inflated by chance/sampling error \u2014 the regression weights capitalized on chance relationships in a (too) small derivation sample relative to the number of predictors, so they do not generalize to a new sample.", "final": "The original multiple R was inflated by chance/sampling error \u2014 the regression weights capitalized on chance relationships in a (too) small derivation sample relative to the number of predictors, so they do not generalize to a new sample."}
{"qid": 295, "answered": null, "resp": "The question omits its answer choices, so I can't identify which specific option is the violation; in general, the most likely ethical violation among typical choices is a professional using confidential client/patient information for personal gain or entering into a dual relationship (e.g., a therapist dating a client), since these breach confidentiality, exploit power imbalances, and impair objectivity. If you share the options, I can pinpoint the correct one.\n\nFINAL: Cannot be determined without the answer choices; the most likely violation would be exploiting a confidential/fiduciary relationship for personal gain (e.g., a therapist entering a romantic or business relationship with a client).", "final": "Cannot be determined without the answer choices; the most likely violation would be exploiting a confidential/fiduciary relationship for personal gain (e.g., a therapist entering a romantic or business relationship with a client)."}
{"qid": 296, "answered": null, "resp": "Berscheid's emotion-in-relationship model holds that emotions arise when a partner's behavior interrupts or disrupts one's organized, well-established sequences of behavior/expectations; in later stages, partners' behaviors have become highly predictable and meshed (interlinked and expected), so there are fewer interruptions and hence fewer strong emotions\u2014positive ones included.\n\nFINAL: Because emotion is triggered by unexpected interruptions of habitual behavior sequences, and in later stages partners' behaviors have become so predictable and well-meshed that the partner rarely does anything unexpected (interruptive) enough to elicit strong emotion.", "final": "Because emotion is triggered by unexpected interruptions of habitual behavior sequences, and in later stages partners' behaviors have become so predictable and well-meshed that the partner rarely does anything unexpected (interruptive) enough to elicit strong emotion."}
{"qid": 297, "answered": null, "resp": "Fromm described several mechanisms of escape from freedom (authoritarianism, destructiveness, automaton conformity) that fail to realize one's true nature; only spontaneous, positive freedom\u2014relating to the world through love and productive work\u2014allows a person to realize their loving, creative, productive nature. This is his \"productive orientation\" (productive character), as opposed to the non-productive orientations (receptive, exploitative, hoarding, marketing).\n\nFINAL: Positive freedom via the productive orientation\u2014responding to society through spontaneous love and productive work (the productive character), rather than the escape mechanisms of authoritarianism, destructiveness, or automaton conformity.", "final": "Positive freedom via the productive orientation\u2014responding to society through spontaneous love and productive work (the productive character), rather than the escape mechanisms of authoritarianism, destructiveness, or automaton conformity."}
{"qid": 298, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "This is a classic psychology question: in cross-sectional designs, differences between age groups may reflect generational (cohort) differences in experience rather than age itself, so the computer-experience difference is a cohort effect.\n\nFINAL: Cohort effect", "final": "Cohort effect"}
{"qid": 299, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "Research (e.g., Carlson et al., 1989) shows that maltreated infants disproportionately display disorganized/disoriented attachment (Type D), characterized by contradictory, fearful, and confused behaviors toward the caregiver who is both a source of comfort and of fear.\n\nFINAL: Disorganized/disoriented attachment (Type D)", "final": "Disorganized/disoriented attachment (Type D)"}
{"qid": 300, "answered": null, "resp": "Validity is the extent to which a test actually measures the construct it claims to measure, as distinct from reliability (consistency).\n\nFINAL: measures what it is intended (purports) to measure", "final": "measures what it is intended (purports) to measure"}
{"qid": 301, "answered": null, "resp": "XXY is Klinefelter syndrome; the presence of the Y chromosome (SRY gene) drives male development, so the fetus will develop as a phenotypic male, typically with small testes, infertility, and possibly some feminized features like reduced body hair or gynecomastia. It is not lethal and does not produce a female phenotype.\n\nFINAL: develop into a male (Klinefelter syndrome) \u2014 a phenotypic male, usually sterile, with small testes and often some feminizing traits.", "final": "develop into a male (Klinefelter syndrome) \u2014 a phenotypic male, usually sterile, with small testes and often some feminizing traits."}
{"qid": 302, "answered": null, "resp": "The left hemisphere is dominant for language functions (speech production, comprehension, reading, writing) as well as analytical, logical, and sequential processing, in contrast to the right hemisphere's specialization in spatial and holistic processing.\n\nFINAL: Language (verbal/linguistic processing) and analytical, logical, sequential reasoning.", "final": "Language (verbal/linguistic processing) and analytical, logical, sequential reasoning."}
{"qid": 303, "answered": null, "resp": "This is a classic psychology example: John has handled money for years but never attended to the details on the faces of coins/bills, so the information was never encoded into long-term memory. His failure is due to lack of encoding (encoding failure) resulting from inattention\u2014not retrieval failure or decay.\n\nFINAL: encoding failure\u2014he never paid attention to the pictures, so the information was never encoded into long-term memory", "final": "encoding failure\u2014he never paid attention to the pictures, so the information was never encoded into long-term memory"}
