{"qid": 489, "answered": null, "resp": "Babbling at 4\u20135 months initially includes a wide range of speech sounds, including those not found in the infant's native language (all phonemes from human languages), which later narrows to native-language sounds.\n\nFINAL: A wide variety of phonemes/speech sounds, including sounds from all human languages (not just the infant's native language), which later narrows to native-language sounds.", "final": "A wide variety of phonemes/speech sounds, including sounds from all human languages (not just the infant's native language), which later narrows to native-language sounds."}
{"qid": 490, "answered": null, "resp": "The Standards emphasize that score interpretations should be supported by validity evidence for the intended use, reported with information about measurement error/precision (e.g., confidence bands), and communicated in a way that is understandable to the intended audience while avoiding overinterpretation of single scores or unsupported inferences.\n\nFINAL: Interpret and report scores only for the purposes and populations for which validity evidence exists, accompany scores with information on their precision (standard error/confidence bands), and communicate results clearly and understandably to the intended audience\u2014cautioning against overinterpretation or misuse of a single score.", "final": "Interpret and report scores only for the purposes and populations for which validity evidence exists, accompany scores with information on their precision (standard error/confidence bands), and communicate results clearly and understandably to the intended audience\u2014cautioning against overinterpretation or misuse of a single score."}
{"qid": 491, "answered": null, "resp": "Wernicke's aphasia (receptive/fluent aphasia) involves poor comprehension while speech remains fluent but is often meaningless (paraphasias, neologisms, \"word salad\"), with impaired repetition and naming.\n\nFINAL: fluent but meaningless (nonsensical) speech \u2014 speech that flows easily but lacks meaning, often with paraphasias and neologisms, and the person is typically unaware of their errors.", "final": "fluent but meaningless (nonsensical) speech \u2014 speech that flows easily but lacks meaning, often with paraphasias and neologisms, and the person is typically unaware of their errors."}
{"qid": 492, "answered": null, "resp": "The overjustification hypothesis (from self-perception theory) holds that when people receive external rewards for an activity they already enjoy, they attribute their behavior to the reward rather than to their own interest, so their intrinsic motivation declines. As a result, once the reward is removed, they engage in the activity less than before.\n\nFINAL: Rewarding someone for doing something they already intrinsically enjoy can undermine their intrinsic motivation\u2014they come to attribute the behavior to the external reward rather than genuine interest, so they enjoy and engage in the activity less (especially once the reward stops).", "final": "Rewarding someone for doing something they already intrinsically enjoy can undermine their intrinsic motivation\u2014they come to attribute the behavior to the external reward rather than genuine interest, so they enjoy and engage in the activity less (especially once the reward stops)."}
{"qid": 493, "answered": null, "resp": "Piaget's preoperational stage (ages 2\u20137) is characterized by the development of symbolic thought\u2014the ability to use words, images, and pretend play to represent objects and events. This emergence of symbolic/representational thinking (including language) is its key accomplishment.\n\nFINAL: Symbolic (representational) thought\u2014the ability to use symbols such as words, images, and pretend play to represent objects.", "final": "Symbolic (representational) thought\u2014the ability to use symbols such as words, images, and pretend play to represent objects."}
{"qid": 494, "answered": null, "resp": "The key distinguishing feature is body weight: anorexia nervosa requires significantly low body weight (below minimally normal), whereas individuals with bulimia nervosa are typically at normal weight or overweight, even though both may involve binge eating and purging.\n\nFINAL: Body weight \u2014 people with bulimia nervosa maintain a normal (or above-normal) body weight, whereas anorexia nervosa is defined by significantly low body weight.", "final": "Body weight \u2014 people with bulimia nervosa maintain a normal (or above-normal) body weight, whereas anorexia nervosa is defined by significantly low body weight."}
{"qid": 495, "answered": null, "resp": "Grade equivalent scores indicate the grade level (year and month) at which the student's raw score is the median/average score of students in the norm group\u2014i.e., the student's raw score equals the typical (median) raw score of students at that grade level, not that the student can do work at that grade level.\n\nFINAL: The grade level (year and month) at which the student's raw score is the median (average) raw score of students in the norm group\u2014i.e., the student performed as well as the typical student at that grade level did on this same test, not that the student has mastered that grade's curriculum or should be placed in that grade.", "final": "The grade level (year and month) at which the student's raw score is the median (average) raw score of students in the norm group\u2014i.e., the student performed as well as the typical student at that grade level did on this same test, not that the student has mastered that grade's curriculum or should be placed in that grade."}
{"qid": 496, "answered": null, "resp": "Administering the same test to the same group at two different times and correlating the scores is the classic method for assessing test-retest reliability, which measures the consistency/stability of a scale's results over time.\n\nFINAL: Assessing the test-retest reliability of his perfectionism scale (checking its consistency over time).", "final": "Assessing the test-retest reliability of his perfectionism scale (checking its consistency over time)."}
